    "md5": "31544cd55a4f5a3619738839c05f53f5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Somaratne, an Australia-based Sri Lankan systems engineer, brings a 30-year career across deep-tech and corporate strategy. A mechatronics engineering graduate of the University of New South Wales, he spent a decade in semiconductor manufacturing and five years in financial services, followed by 15 years in management consulting. That included leading the strategy, transformation and innovation practices at Wipro and DXC Consulting.\n\nSomaratne acknowledged that he independently engineered Sovereign Stack\u2019s bare-metal execution engine, which runs AI directly on the processor without the layers of software abstraction common in today\u2019s AI tools. The codebase spans more than one million lines across 108 repositories on the company\u2019s private servers, with a three-member team supporting testing, hardware design and supply chain coordination.\n\nSomaratne described the outflow as an extractive drain on the domestic economy.\n\n\u201cThe way the digital economy in America is working at the moment is like a parasitic wasp,\u201d he said.\n\nIn a white paper last year, he estimated Australia risked losing about A$ 0.8 trillion over five years, representing around 30 percent of its GDP.\n\nSomaratne dismissed the push for hyperscale data centres as an unsustainable model built to preserve the recurring cloud revenues.\n\n\u201cWe don\u2019t need these massive data centres to be built,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cThis whole question about we need gigawatts and gigawatts of power to run AI, it completely collapses on its own.\u201d\n\nInstead, the framework relies on decentralised PicoDC edge networks\u2014ultra-low-power computing nodes running on solar panels and battery storage that place no additional demand on the grid.\n\nThe nodes will be co-located at renewable power sites to create \u201cCognitive Power Plants\u201d that sell computing capacity alongside electricity."
