    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "25 nations, 5,000+ runners hit Colombo streets\n\nColombo, Sep. 13 (Daily Mirror) - The Colombo Marathon 2026 concluded successfully today (September 13) at Galle Face Green in Colombo, attracting more than 5,000 local and international runners.\n\nThe international event, jointly organized by the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports and the Department of Sports Development, featured more than 130 international athletes representing 25 countries.\n\nA total prize fund of more than Rs. 12.8 million was awarded to winners across several race categories.\n\nUganda\u2019s Julius Ugandan Ochieng won the men\u2019s 42km Full Marathon, completing the race in 2 hours, 18 minutes and 54 seconds. He received the top prize of Rs. 1.7 million.\n\nKenya\u2019s Geoffrey Birgen finished second in 2:20:43, while Ethiopia\u2019s Negasi Tuji Korme placed third with a time of 2:21:50.\n\nEthiopian runners dominated the women\u2019s 42km Full Marathon. Aynalem Kasahun Teferi secured first place in 2:54:09 and won Rs. 1.7 million. Gete Guta Galato finished second in 2:54:48, followed by Genzeb Shumi Regasa in 2:55:20.\n\nIn the men\u2019s 21km Half Marathon, Kenya\u2019s Edwin Yebei Kiptoo took first place with a time of 1:10:28 and received Rs. 500,000. Sri Lanka\u2019s W.M.S. Sarathkumara finished second in 1:10:52, while Kenya\u2019s Bomer Kiptoo Evans placed third in 1:10:53.\n\nSri Lankan athletes dominated the women\u2019s 21km event. Wijesuriya Arachchilage Methmi Rosata won first place in 1:17:50, followed by H.M.C.S. Herath (Chamini) in 1:26:14. Kenya\u2019s Jackline Jeptanui finished third in 1:26:41.\n\nSri Lankan runners also secured all three podium places in both the men\u2019s and women\u2019s 10km races.\n\nMahendran Gobinathan won the men\u2019s 10km in 32:16, followed by R.M. Samantha Pushpakumara in 33:04 and Senanayake D.P.S.S. in 33:06.\n\nIn the women\u2019s 10km, P.G.S.S. Wimalasooriya took first place in 39:08. Oshadini Fonseka finished second in 39:27, while K.M. Shashikala placed third in 42:17."
