    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Our companies invest heavily in infrastructure, technology, human resources and corporate social responsibility. There is also a strong case for investing in knowledge infrastructure, institutions that examine the country\u2019s future and help develop solutions to complex national challenges. This becomes even more important when we consider the challenges facing Sri Lanka. The country cannot approach maritime affairs, regional connectivity, national security, food security, disaster resilience, financial crime or human trafficking as completely separate issues. They increasingly intersect.\n\nSri Lankan universities, think tanks, chambers, professional bodies and Government institutions could work together to develop research and policy initiatives linked to BIMSTEC priorities. The Ceylon Chamber of Shipping could potentially contribute particularly strongly to this effort by providing a private-sector and maritime perspective to regional discussions. Such engagement would also strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s soft power. Countries do not build influence only through military capability or economic size. They build influence through ideas, institutions, expertise and the ability to convene others. Sri Lanka has the intellectual resources to do this. What is required is a greater institutional commitment.\n\nMy meeting with Ambassador Pandey therefore left me with a broader thought: Sri Lanka\u2019s engagement with BIMSTEC should not be viewed simply through the lens of foreign policy. It should be treated as part of a wider national strategy involving maritime development, economic cooperation, regional security, knowledge creation and policy innovation.\n\nThe opportunity is there. BIMSTEC is increasingly relevant to the strategic and economic future of the Bay of Bengal region. Sri Lanka, with its unique geographical position and maritime heritage, has every reason to play a more active role. But to do so effectively, we need more than diplomatic participation."
