    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "The opportunity is there. BIMSTEC is increasingly relevant to the strategic and economic future of the Bay of Bengal region. Sri Lanka, with its unique geographical position and maritime heritage, has every reason to play a more active role. But to do so effectively, we need more than diplomatic participation.\n\nThe conversation with Ambassador Pandey was encouraging because it demonstrated that there is international recognition of the importance of such engagement. His interest in closer cooperation with the Ceylon Chamber of Shipping and his intention to continue the dialogue during his next visit in November provide a useful starting point. The next step should be to turn that conversation into action. Sri Lanka should build stronger bridges between BIMSTEC, the maritime sector, the private sector, universities and think tanks. If we can do that, we will not merely participate in the changing regional order, we can contribute to shaping it.\n\nFor a country positioned at the heart of the Indian Ocean, that should be an ambition worth pursuing.\n\n(The author is the Secretary General of the Ceylon Chamber of Shipping, former Director General of the Institute of National Security Studies (INSS), and former Spokesman of the Ministry of Defence. He is also a Non-Resident Fellow at the London Dialogue. His areas of expertise include international relations, geopolitics, maritime affairs, national security, economic policy, and strategic governance. He is a consultant at JIT Group)"
