    "md5": "7ea074be0240d8a2490aa43cd27c5356",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "Former Board Director Professor Sonal Fernando has offered another perspective on aircraft procurement. He argued that commissions paid to buyers can be incorporated into aircraft pricing and claimed a transaction could become void if a buyer refused a seller\u2019s commission. He noted that aircraft can be purchased directly from manufacturers, through authorised representatives, or via brokers\u2014arguing that intermediaries can sometimes be commercially advantageous. Describing the international aircraft market as controlled by a \u201cmafia,\u201d Prof. Fernando criticised SriLankan Airlines\u2019 decision to pursue the A350 procurement as foolish.\n\nThe present allegations against Rajapaksa appear to arise from evidence obtained through a subsequent investigation. Following an inquiry that included an affidavit from businessman Nimal Perera, the Colombo Chief Magistrate\u2019s Court was told that Perera alleged he handed Rs. 100 million to Rajapaksa in connection with funds received through the Airbus deal. That remains an allegation. Perera\u2019s credibility and evidentiary value must ultimately be determined through the judicial process.\n\nDeputy Solicitor General Janaka Bandara told court that Rajapaksa\u2019s arrest followed a joint investigation by CIABOC and the CID, with help from authorities in Australia, Singapore, France, and the United States. According to court submissions, \u20ac1.45 million had allegedly been paid by Airbus in connection with the transaction. Investigators examined funds originating from a Singapore bank account under \u201cBiz Solution,\u201d owned by Priyanka Niyomali Wijenayake, wife of former CEO Kapila Chandrasena. Funds from that account were transferred to a Singapore account belonging to a company chaired by Nimal Perera.\n\nThese transactions may form an evidentiary chain, but prosecutors must establish a direct, provable connection between the money and the alleged payment to Rajapaksa."
