    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "In a recent interview with Daily Mirror, MP Dilith Jayaweera said that Sarvajana Balaya\u2019s vision is to govern rather than act as a political power broker for major political parties. Drawing on his background as an entrepreneur and brand strategist, Jayaweera acknowledged that he isn\u2019t happy about his political campaign. He underscored that fact that lasting support cannot be built on media presence alone though it could attract attention for a short period of time. Instead, he believes that earning public trust through hands-on experience as a long-term challenge; one he believes he will achieve by cultivating strong, youth-led second-tier leadership. The Parliamentarian stated that as a new political party he is happy with the party\u2019s progress. He further said that NPP is governing the country in the most archaic manner and added that bringing 22nd Amendment would be disastrous and would result repercussions which would affect the country\u2019s democracy.\n\nMy entry into politics itself is different from the other politicians who took to politics. I decided to get into active politics due to the fact that I saw a huge gap. Traditionally Sri Lankan politics grew through these traditional political parties. People enter politics through them. When I first entered politics, the first challenge for someone like me, who has not been in politics, was how to make myself relevant in the sphere of politics.\n\nI had a few choices. First to go on my own without any support from any traditional political party. Second was to make a noticeable entry with the support of some existing political parties and politicians. That way I will be seen as serious contender than an ad hoc overnight political entrant.\n\nTo answer your question- when I had to work with, for example the Communist Party and whether it clashes with the concept of entrepreneurship and entrepreneurial state-in today\u2019s context socialism does not clash with these two."
  },
