    "md5": "b993ef432b19b5a0088abce177c42c48",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Pick a Book steps beyond reading to preserve the human edge in AI age\n\nFor seven years, we asked people to pick a book. Now we are asking a nation to pick each other up - one reader, one club, one story at a time\n\nCan AI bring the joy and imagination that a book gives? Can it provide the warmth of a conversation that brings soul-to-soul connection? Can it inspire a love of reading and, most importantly, can it give us the smell of a new book?\n\nThese questions were among those raised at the recently held Readers\u2019 Summit 2026, organised by Pick a Book under the theme \u2018Human edge begins with reading\u2019, which brought writers, leading entrepreneurs, readers and publishers together.\n\nPick a Book Sri Lanka, a seven-year-old reading movement, is now moving beyond book clubs, seeking to build a national ecosystem of readers, authors, publishers and booksellers as artificial intelligence transforms how people access information.\n\nWhen was the last time you had a real conversation \u2014 not an exchange on Instagram or Facebook, or even a voice message, but a conversation that began with an idea and went somewhere?\n\nThat was the question posed by Barista Coffee Lanka Director and CEO Dilupa Pathirana, at the Readers\u2019 Summit in Colombo, as he explored the relationship between reading, knowledge and human connection.\n\n\u201cBecause for a conversation, you need a topic,\u201d Pathirana said, adding that the material for that conversation comes from what people have listened to, experienced and, ultimately, read.\n\nIn an age in which people are bombarded with information, his message gives greater meaning to the objective of the summit, which promotes the idea that reading matters not simply because books contain knowledge, but because they give people something to think about and talk about.\n\n\u201cWe are having a conversation with somebody who lived 500 years ago,\u201d Pathirana said, describing the extraordinary reach created by writing and books."
  },
