    "chunk_text": "The first area focuses on engineering, technology and technical education. Sri Lanka needs an education and professional development system that responds to national needs while maintaining internationally recognised standards. The proposed policy seeks clearer categories for Engineers, Engineering Technologists and Engineering Technicians, together with defined competencies, qualifications and career pathways. It also highlights the importance of accreditation, workforce planning and stronger links between education, industry and professional practice. A well-planned system can provide young people with clearer opportunities while ensuring that the country develops the skills needed for future growth.\n\nTransport is the third priority. The proposal is to establish an integrated multimodal people-centred public transport system that provides fast, affordable, reliable, and seamless connectivity between cities, townships, economic centres, and rural nodes. The proposal considers an integrated fare and ticketing system, with first- and last-mile connectivity embedded into a national digital platform. Expected national-level benefits include reduced travel time, manageable congestion, reduced fuel consumption, lower logistics costs, enhanced productivity and improved regional connectivity.\n\nThe fourth area addresses building fire safety. Sri Lanka currently has fragmented responsibilities and no single national authority responsible for the continuous regulation of building fire safety. The proposed framework calls for a National Building Fire Safety Act and an independent National Fire Safety Authority. It also proposes regular inspections, certification, clear technical standards, professional review and effective enforcement. These measures are intended to protect lives and property and create consistent safety standards across the country."
  },
