    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5ba8139bbf68a2e5a5f91d5c7f60270a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "Plaid has ruled out an independence referendum before the next Senedd election in 2030.\n\nAt the press conference, ap Iorwerth said there was a \"growing recognition that Westminster is not working\" and \"power genuinely needs to be brought closer to people and communities\".\n\n\"Wales should have the power, the resources, the fair treatment we need as a nation to build the future our people deserve,\" he said.\n\nSNP leader Swinney said Monday was a \"pivotal moment in the constitutional journey of the United Kingdom\".\n\nHe said the elections in May for each nation created a situation with first ministers who believe \"their countries should decide their own future\".\n\n\"That principle of self-determination is an inalienable right,\" he said.\n\nHe described Andy Burnham as the \"last prime minister of the United Kingdom\".\n\n\"Because the people of Scotland, if given the choice of independence in a referendum will take that choice.\"\n\nSinn F\u00e9in's McDonald described Monday's meeting as \"historic\", saying: \"We have the right to referendum. We have the right to decide our future.\n\n\"We will not accept or tolerate any prime minister telling us that right is off the table.\"\n\nNorthern Ireland's First Minister O'Neill said all three nations were \"distinctively different\" and had their own priorities.\n\nBut she said the \"common cause here is that Westminster does not and will never serve our people well\".\n\nO'Neill serves in a power sharing executive in Northern Ireland, where the Democratic Unionist Party's (DUP) deputy first minister Emma Little-Pengelly accused the Sinn Fein politician of attempting to \"weaponise the role of first minister\".\n\nIn Northern Ireland, the first and deputy first minister have identical powers.\n\nAsked by BBC Wales to clarify what the memorandum of understanding meant for Wales, and whether it meant asking Westminster not to block a Welsh independence referendum, ap Iorwerth said it was \"up to the people of Wales to decide its own future\"."
  },
  {
