    "description": "BRICS: While others lobbied,Sri Lanka lost ground",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/BRICS-While-others-lobbied-Sri-Lanka-lost-ground/108-350314",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789452566.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-c8b354b4-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "adc456f061ced0c00982beb4ca5ca44e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1646,
    "chunk_text": "BRICS: While others lobbied,Sri Lanka lost ground\n\nAs leaders of some of the world\u2019s most influential emerging economies gathered in New Delhi for the 18th BRICS Summit last weekend at Bharat Mandapam once again took centre stage in global diplomacy.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, however, the summit should trigger an uncomfortable question. How did a country that formally sought BRICS membership end up watching from the sidelines while Uganda, Nigeria, Malaysia, Thailand, Vietnam and others moved ahead as BRICS Partner Countries?\n\nSri Lanka wanted a seat at the table. Others secured one. We did not. This is not the time for excuses or bureaucratic silence. Policymakers must identify where the system failed, correct it and act.\n\nIn global diplomacy, opportunities lost today may not return tomorrow.\n\nBRICS is not simply another diplomatic gathering. India\u2019s 2026 Chairship has last weekend involved hundreds of meetings and engagements covering political cooperation, finance, trade, innovation, business and people-to-people exchanges. For Sri Lanka, closer engagement could mean greater access to investment, export markets, development finance, technology, energy cooperation and some of the world\u2019s fastest-growing economies.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s interest was clear. In 2024, it is reported the then Government examined joining BRICS and the New Development Bank (NDB) and approved proceeding with applications. Following the change of government, it is reported that the current Foreign Minister Vijitha Herath communicated Sri Lanka\u2019s interest to BRICS counterparts, while President Anura Kumara Dissanayake reportedly sought Russian support."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-c8b354b4",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-c8b354b4",
    "date_str": "2026-09-15",
    "description": "BRICS: While others lobbied,Sri Lanka lost ground",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/BRICS-While-others-lobbied-Sri-Lanka-lost-ground/108-350314",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789452566.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-c8b354b4-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cf358459abd6218889b100dea5143aa1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s interest was clear. In 2024, it is reported the then Government examined joining BRICS and the New Development Bank (NDB) and approved proceeding with applications. Following the change of government, it is reported that the current Foreign Minister Vijitha Herath communicated Sri Lanka\u2019s interest to BRICS counterparts, while President Anura Kumara Dissanayake reportedly sought Russian support.\n\nSri Lanka therefore cannot claim it was absent from the process. But it certainly cannot be satisfied with the result.\n\nIn June 2025, the Russian Ambassador in Colombo publicly urged Sri Lanka to take \u201cpractical steps\u201d if it genuinely wanted to join BRICS, while indicating that Russia and China were supportive.\n\nThis statement should have set alarm bells ringing in Colombo.\n\nBRICS subsequently expanded its Partner Country framework. Belarus, Bolivia, Cuba, Kazakhstan, Malaysia, Thailand, Uganda, Uzbekistan and Nigeria became partners, with Vietnam joining later.\n\nSri Lanka was not among them. There has been no public indication that Sri Lanka's membership application was formally rejected. But that makes the question even more important.\n\nThe President was interested. The Foreign Minister was interested. The Government wanted closer BRICS engagement.\n\nPolitical interest without bureaucratic execution produces little. Diplomacy is not about sending letters and waiting for replies. It is about knocking on doors, building support country by country and following through relentlessly until the national objective is achieved.\n\nWas BRICS treated as a genuine foreign-policy priority? Were India, China, Russia, Brazil, South Africa and the newer members systematically engaged? Were Sri Lankan missions given a coordinated lobbying strategy? Did we explain not merely why Sri Lanka wants BRICS, but why BRICS should want Sri Lanka?\n\nOther countries of different sizes, regions and economic weight secured Partner Country status. They advanced. Sri Lanka did not."
  },
