    "md5": "143276ff9135eb0283ed1a81bb6ce517",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "Strong remittances offer FX cushion as rupee comes under pressure\n\nThe workers\u2019 remittances remained robust in August, providing a key foreign exchange cushion as the rupee continued to face depreciation pressure.\n\nThe remittance inflows reached US $ 748.6 million in August 2026, up nearly 10 percent from US $ 680.8 million a year earlier, according to the latest Central Bank data.\n\nThe strong year-on-year performance comes as the rupee has weakened against the US dollar, recording a 5.7 percent year-to-date depreciation as of September 11.\n\nWhile the August inflows moderated by 3.7 percent from US $ 777.6 million in July, they remained at a relatively high level, reinforcing the remittances as an important source of foreign currency for the economy.\n\nThe Central Bank noted that the Brent and WTI crude prices surged during the week ended September 11, on concerns over the prolonged Middle East conflicts and potential supply disruptions. Both benchmarks had moved above US $ 100 per barrel by the end of the period.\n\nThe higher oil prices could increase Sri Lanka\u2019s import bill and foreign exchange demand, making the steady inflows from the remittances increasingly important in supporting the balance of payments and easing the pressure in the domestic foreign exchange market.\n\nMeanwhile, the gross official reserves were provisionally estimated at US $ 6.905 billion at end-August 2026, inclusive of the proceeds from the People\u2019s Bank of China swap arrangement.\n\nThe workers\u2019 remittances, together with the export and tourism receipts, remain among Sri Lanka\u2019s principal sources of foreign exchange earnings."
  },
