    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ceec92e5a42f3945e789f441a40448b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe purpose of the measure is to facilitate the collection of the outstanding revenue,\u201d EY said, adding that this would be best achieved through an efficient administrative process that allows the taxpayers to regularise their affairs before the concession expires.\n\nThe concession applies to the assessments relating to the years of assessment up to and including 2024/25, that are subject to pending tax audits, requests for administrative review or appeals.\n\nEY noted that the IRD has already instructed the tax officials to expedite the pending audits to allow the taxpayers to qualify for the relief. It said similar urgency was needed for pending administrative reviews, penalty remission applications, ongoing appeals and confirmation of relief granted.\n\nIt suggested that a dedicated administrative framework, standard application procedures, published turnaround times and expedited decision-making could improve compliance and revenue collection.\n\n\u201cThe objective should be to facilitate settlement rather than create procedural roadblocks that prevent the taxpayers from accessing the concession Parliament intended to provide,\u201d EY said.\n\nSignificantly, the taxpayers with assessments under dispute may also be able to secure the interest waiver without abandoning their appeal rights.\n\nAccording to EY, the IRD guidance indicates that the taxpayers may pay the principal tax and applicable penalty within the amnesty period to qualify for the interest waiver while continuing to challenge the assessment through the available review and appeal mechanisms.\n\nThis could allow the taxpayers to halt further accumulation of interest while an appeal proceeds. If the taxpayer ultimately succeeds, the IRD guidelines provide a mechanism to seek a refund of the tax paid, although EY noted that the legislation itself does not specifically address this situation."
