    "time_ut": 1789453115.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-090e0443-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8af2fbc2ab997e5d288011d1e54d7349",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1974,
    "chunk_text": "Retailers urged to go regional as uneven recovery leaves households behind\n\nPoverty still double pre-crisis levels; real wages falling even as per capita GDP\n\nRetailers squeezed between energy, tax and import costs and shoppers, who now compare prices online and in-store\n\nAsset-light overseas models, FDI and private equity flagged as route past a 22mn-consumer ceiling\n\nThe Sri Lankan retailers and consumer brands must look beyond the confines of the domestic market, adopt asset-light international expansion strategies and tap foreign private capital to achieve sustainable growth, as the country\u2019s macroeconomic stabilisation is yet to translate into a broad-based recovery for the households, the keynote speakers told Sri Lanka Retail Forum 2026.\n\nDelivering a keynote address on macroeconomic productivity and retail bottlenecks at the forum, organised by the Sri Lanka Retailers\u2019 Association, in Colombo, under the theme \u2018Retail Without Boundaries: Building the Next Growth Engine\u2019 yesterday, Centre for Poverty Analysis Consultant Dr. Roshan Perera pointed out that while the headline economic indicators have stabilised over the past three years, a significant divide persists between stabilisation and tangible economic expansion.\n\nShe noted that while the GDP per capita has returned to pre-crisis levels, the post-crisis rebound has been uneven, leaving the consumers with diminished purchasing power.\n\n\u201cPoverty has come down from where we were but it\u2019s still double what we were prior to the crisis. There are still more than one in five people who are still living below the poverty line and the real wages have actually fallen, even as the per capita GDP has increased,\u201d Dr. Perera said.\n\n\u201cWhat this implies really is that this recovery has not been necessarily evenly shared across the households. That\u2019s something probably you retailers will feel, that the spending power or spending capacity of the households has probably declined.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-090e0443",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-090e0443",
    "date_str": "2026-09-15",
    "description": "Retailers urged to go regional as uneven recovery leaves households behind",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Retailers-urged-to-go-regional-as-uneven-recovery-leaves-households-behind/273-350323",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789453115.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-090e0443-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ca60010775001bec28e89809b377e3c9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1769,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWhat this implies really is that this recovery has not been necessarily evenly shared across the households. That\u2019s something probably you retailers will feel, that the spending power or spending capacity of the households has probably declined.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe worrying thing is that core inflation, which sort of strips out fuel and food prices, is also moving above this 5 percent target. That is worrying because it suggests the pickup is not just in terms of food and fuel but it is a bit more broad-based and this will obviously matter directly for your own retail input costs and also for how long the Central Bank can keep the policy rates on hold,\u201d she warned.\n\nDr. Perera stressed that the retailers are caught between the escalating external overheads such as the elevated energy tariffs, taxation and import costs and constrained domestic pricing power. Addressing whether the retailers can pass these rising expenses onto the buyers, she noted that the consumer dynamics have fundamentally shifted.\n\n\u201cI think the retailers are now very conscious of value and I don\u2019t think you can just pass the costs to the consumers because there\u2019s much more opportunity to shop around. It\u2019s not just physical stores; there are online stores. Being more value-conscious, reducing costs, making your operations more efficient would be important for the retailers if you want to keep your customers,\u201d she said.\n\nWith Sri Lanka\u2019s consumer base limited to just 22 million people, smaller than metropolitan Mumbai, Dr. Perera urged the retailers to tap regional integration, leverage the bilateral free trade agreements and plug into the regional value chains, pointing to India\u2019s over 400-million-strong middle class as an accessible supply and consumer base."
  },
