    "md5": "da06a8cb5a4e51caecb03caf9621b743",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "According to Dr. Nanayakkara, the powers previously available under the 1894 Rabies Ordinance to kill animals found on the streets have been abolished.\n\n\u201cA dog cannot simply be killed because it has bitten someone or is suspected of having rabies,\u201d he said.\n\nEuthanasia would only be permitted where a government veterinarian officially confirms that the dog has rabies and determines that it is necessary for public safety, he said.\n\nThe Act also requires sterilisation of stray dogs to be carried out by veterinarians. Dogs undergoing sterilisation must be cared for until they recover and then returned to the location from which they were captured, with an identifying mark to prevent them from being captured again.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara said people who abandon dogs in public places, including near temples, could face fines of Rs. 10,000 or more and imprisonment ranging from three to six months.\n\nThe legislation also introduces regulations for commercial dog breeders, requiring breeding establishments to obtain licences and provide adequate food, water, shelter, ventilation, lighting, exercise and veterinary care.\n\nSri Lanka has an estimated 2.1 million dogs, although only around three per cent are believed to be genuinely ownerless street dogs, Dr. Nanayakkara said.\n\nHe identified irresponsible waste disposal and abandonment of animals as major contributors to the country\u2019s stray dog problem.\n\nHe said sterilisation and vaccination were essential to addressing the problem and achieving the goal of eliminating rabies by 2030.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara claimed that funds allocated for sterilisation had not been fully utilised, with around Rs. 100 million of the Rs. 200 million allocated in 2024 being spent, while only about Rs. 48 million had been utilised from the 2025 allocation.\n\nHe said arrangements were also being finalised for the Government and private sector to provide free rabies vaccination and sterilisation services for both stray and owned dogs."
