    "md5": "02645d5718e87ed8ba236cc40ebfef90",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "IESL National Engineering Conference 2026 to focus on 4 national priorities\n\nTo bring engineering, policy and industry leaders together for Sri Lanka\u2019s future\n\nThe Institution of Engineers Sri Lanka (IESL) will hold National Engineering Conference 2026 (NEC 2026) on September 24, 2026, at 3:00 p.m., at Cinnamon Grand Colombo.\n\nThe conference will bring together the policymakers, senior government officials, engineers, academics, industry leaders, investors and development partners to discuss practical solutions for several important national challenges.\n\nNEC 2026 is designed as more than a professional conference. The IESL will present four policy papers prepared by the engineering professionals and invite the relevant authorities and stakeholders to consider practical actions for implementation.\n\nThe four areas are engineering education, energy transition and power sector reforms, transport sector development and building fire safety.\n\nThe first area focuses on engineering, technology and technical education. Sri Lanka needs an education and professional development system that responds to the national needs while maintaining the internationally recognised standards.\n\nThe proposed policy seeks clearer categories for the engineers, engineering technologists and engineering technicians, together with defined competencies, qualifications and career pathways.\n\nIt also highlights the importance of accreditation, workforce planning and stronger links between education, industry and professional practice. A well-planned system can provide young people with clearer opportunities while ensuring that the country develops the skills needed for future growth.\n\nThe second area is energy transition and power sector reform. The report finds that the main challenge is now implementation rather than absence of policy."
  },
