    "md5": "f1bd4ffe08df4d5e9f85339bb796e475",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Services surge as manufacturing loses steam in August\n\nEconomic activity displayed mixed momentum in August, with the services sector accelerating sharply while manufacturing growth slowed as production contracted and new orders stagnated, the latest Central Bank Purchasing Managers\u2019 Index (PMI) showed.\n\nThe Services PMI rose to 65.6 in August from 61.4 in July, signalling a faster expansion in business activity, while the Manufacturing PMI slipped to 53.0 from 55.0, though remaining above the 50-point threshold that separates expansion from contraction.\n\nThe manufacturing reading masked greater weakness within the sector. Production fell into contraction territory, with the sub-index dropping to 48.4 from 54.5, while New Orders declined to 50.0 from 55.9, indicating broadly unchanged new business during the month.\n\nThe Central Bank said the moderation in production was driven by the textiles and wearing apparel sector.\n\nHowever, some manufacturing indicators remained encouraging. Employment strengthened to 54.8 from 51.0, while stocks of purchases increased to 54.8 from 51.5, reflecting continued recruitment and a gradual inventory build-up ahead of year-end seasonal production.\n\nSupply conditions remained a concern, with the Suppliers\u2019 Delivery Times index rising to 63.0 from 61.5, reflecting what the Central Bank described as \u201cpersistent supply disruptions\u201d.\n\nDespite the slowdown, manufacturers remained positive about the months ahead, largely on expectations of the customary year-end seasonal pick-up, though the Central Bank said near-term challenges continued to temper the outlook.\n\nIn contrast, services gathered considerable momentum during August, with stronger activity recorded across several key segments of the economy.\n\nThe Central Bank said the expansion was primarily powered by transportation, wholesale and retail trade, and professional services, with financial and other personal services also making notable contributions."
