    "md5": "8fc7461a8e9593a4f5157993b1333d2c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1420,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka secures 7.5% weight in JP Morgan\u2019s new frontier local currency bond index\n\nSL\u2019s allocation sits half a percentage point below the ceiling on any single country\n\nMany of the markets with the largest weightings, including Sri Lanka, have staged strong rallies in recent years following crises\n\nEconomists expect the index to help deepen local currency debt markets\n\nSri Lanka has been assigned a 7.5 percent weighting in J.P. Morgan\u2019s new frontier market local currency government bond index, placing it among the eight largest components of a benchmark set to track close to US$ 330 billion of debt across 26 countries, according to details of the index released this week.\n\nThe Wall Street bank will launch the GBI-EM Edge by the end of September, a J.P. Morgan note to investors seen by Reuters showed. The benchmark arrives almost two decades after the bank\u2019s hard-currency NEXGEM frontier index and spans markets ranging from Albania to Zambia.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s allocation sits half a percentage point below the ceiling on any single country. Vietnam, Egypt, Morocco, Pakistan, Bangladesh and Kazakhstan each carry the full 8 percent weighting, with Nigeria close behind Sri Lanka at 7.4 percent, as per J.P. Morgan\u2019s index research dated September 14. Taken together, the seven markets at or near the cap make up 55.5 percent of the index, a share that climbs to 62.9 percent once Nigeria is added."
  },
