    "chunk_id": "2026-09-16-dailymirrorlk-359fe019-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a3861e1677e77823bb2c3bca5b525bb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "During a discussion with the heads of the Department of Immigration and Emigration, Sri Lanka Customs, and Airport and Aviation Services, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake also highlighted early last year the need to eliminate negative perceptions surrounding Sri Lanka Customs and underscored the urgency of implementing strict disciplinary and legal action to restore public confidence.\n\nGunasena cannot lay claim to this statement either. Nandana Gunathilaka, a former JVP stalwart who later turned to be its strong advesary, said in\u00a0 reply to the laments by Lalkantha and Handunnetti that if the JVP\u2019s assumption of power had been a revolutionary capture of power, it would have brought the state machinery under total control of the party.\n\n\u201cFollowing a non-revolutionary capture of power nothing more could be done, except for working within the existing system. Yet, revolutionary changes have to be brought in, in order to keep the promises given to the people, \u201d Gunathilake observed.\n\nA democratic transfer of power through elections means that the state machinery which is largely corrupt and lethargic would be intact and unchanged, even after the election. Former President Maithripala Sirisena, addressing the 45th National Conference of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Sri Lanka (CA Sri Lanka) in October 2024,\u00a0 vividly explained how corruption is rampant within the state machinery,\n\nHe said that Ministers cannot steal without the knowledge of their secretaries and chief accountants. He also noted that after witnessing the minister\u2019s behaviour, these high-level officials often begin to follow the minister\u2019s example, a corrupt practice that eventually trickles down to lower-level employees within the ministry."
  },
