    "description": "Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s Airbus scandal:  SRI LANKAN  POLITICAL SCRIPT  OF MAKING  A HERO  OUT OF A  CROOK",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Namal-Rajapaksas-Airbus-scandal-SRI-LANKAN-POLITICAL-SCRIPT-OF-MAKING-A-HERO-OUT-OF-A-CROOK/172-350406",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789509549.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-16-dailymirrorlk-e6065df1-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3ad5511a0e188f625a97e7b15099d003",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s Airbus scandal:  SRI LANKAN  POLITICAL SCRIPT  OF MAKING  A HERO  OUT OF A  CROOK\n\nThe government, however, has a duty to defend the integrity of the judicial process and the credibility of the CIABOC and other investigating apparatus\n\nNamal Rajapaksa, an SLPP lawmaker and the heir apparent to the Rajapaksa throne, was remanded on Sept. 04 by the Colombo Chief Magistrate\u2019s Court for allegedly receiving 100 million rupees as a bribe in connection with a controversial Airbus procurement deal in 2013.\n\nThe probe, launched by the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery and Corruption (CIABOC), is a rare case in which the state apparatus has decisively pursued a high-profile allegation of corruption linked to the former regime heavyweights.\n\nThe case is important in many respects. First is the extent of corruption itself and its wider implications \u2013 not simply the two million US dollars in bribes allegedly received by local politicians and Sri Lankan airlines officials. Sri Lanka later paid $ 115million as a cancellation penalty for the same corrupt procurement contract, and overpaid US$ 50 million for two old aircraft procured under the same deal.\n\nSecond is the impunity with which how the old regime actors had long protected their own, behind a brief court appearance scripted for public consumption. The allegations first came to light in 2020 as part of a French, US and British investigation into alleged practices of bribery and corruption in Airbus, which later agreed to pay a $ 4billion fine in settlement.\n\nThird is the regular Sri Lankan political script of how to make a hero \u2014or a political victim\u2014 out of a crook. That melodrama is currently in play."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-16-dailymirrorlk-e6065df1",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-e6065df1",
    "date_str": "2026-09-16",
    "description": "Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s Airbus scandal:  SRI LANKAN  POLITICAL SCRIPT  OF MAKING  A HERO  OUT OF A  CROOK",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Namal-Rajapaksas-Airbus-scandal-SRI-LANKAN-POLITICAL-SCRIPT-OF-MAKING-A-HERO-OUT-OF-A-CROOK/172-350406",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789509549.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-16-dailymirrorlk-e6065df1-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c3d9a08680fbb5048f50348ce5a4ffed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "Second is the impunity with which how the old regime actors had long protected their own, behind a brief court appearance scripted for public consumption. The allegations first came to light in 2020 as part of a French, US and British investigation into alleged practices of bribery and corruption in Airbus, which later agreed to pay a $ 4billion fine in settlement.\n\nThird is the regular Sri Lankan political script of how to make a hero \u2014or a political victim\u2014 out of a crook. That melodrama is currently in play.\n\nFirst, about the corruption itself. In 2013, Sri Lankan Airlines had reached an agreement to purchase and lease a total of 16 aircraft from France\u2019s Airbus. The CIABOC alleged there had been an agreement between the two parties to pay a USD 1 million bribe for each aircraft. Subsequently, after the purchase of two aircraft, Airbus transferred nearly 1.45 million euros (then equivalent to USD 2 million) to an account of BIZ Solutions Inc maintained at the Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. The investigators found the parent company of BIZ Solutions Inc was registered in Brunei, with Priyanka Niyomali Wijenayake as the sole director, the wife of former Sri Lankan Airlines Chief Executive Officer Kapila Chandrasena.\n\nAccording to the submissions made before the court, 400,000 euro and AU$ 200,000 had been transferred from the BIZ Solution Inc account to a personal account of Kapila Chandrasena maintained at the Commonwealth Bank of Australia. Another 168,400 euros from the same account had been transferred to a Singaporean company named \u2018SL Agro,\u2019 which then had transferred Rs. 9 million, Rs. 7 million and Rs. 2 million on three occasions to a company called \u201cSasisi Media\u201d owned by Shamendra Rajapaksa, the youngest son of former Speaker of Parliament, Chamal Rajapaksa."
  },
