    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "According to the submissions made before the court, 400,000 euro and AU$ 200,000 had been transferred from the BIZ Solution Inc account to a personal account of Kapila Chandrasena maintained at the Commonwealth Bank of Australia. Another 168,400 euros from the same account had been transferred to a Singaporean company named \u2018SL Agro,\u2019 which then had transferred Rs. 9 million, Rs. 7 million and Rs. 2 million on three occasions to a company called \u201cSasisi Media\u201d owned by Shamendra Rajapaksa, the youngest son of former Speaker of Parliament, Chamal Rajapaksa.\n\nInvestigators also discovered that US$ 800,000 in two separate payments had been transferred from the BIZ Solutions account to an account maintained at OCBC Bank of Singapore for a company named Sabre Vision Holdings Ltd, owned by businessman Nimal Perera.\n\nInvestigators had visited Perera, who currently resides in Australia. Perera has refused to return to Sri Lanka, and instead has given an affidavit in which he claimed that he had personally handed over RS 100 million to Namal Rajapaksa in cash.\n\nAirbus has cooperated with the Sri Lankan investigation, and its officials have visited Sri Lanka on three occasions, given four statements, and handed over all official documents, emails and bank account records pertaining to local transactions.\n\nPriyanka Niyomali Wijenayake had jumped bail and is currently absconding from the court and living in Australia. Shamendra Rajapaksa is living in the US, and Nimal Perera is in Australia. The court was informed that steps had been underway to bring Wijenayake and Rajapaksa to Sri Lanka.\n\nHowever, much of the weight of Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s indictment lies in the credibility of the affidavit of Nimal Perera, who was a close Rajapaksa associate. The investigators have curiously failed to hold Nimal Perera to account for receiving $ 800,000 into a bank account owned by him."
  },
