    "chunk_size_bytes": 1749,
    "chunk_text": "However, much of the weight of Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s indictment lies in the credibility of the affidavit of Nimal Perera, who was a close Rajapaksa associate. The investigators have curiously failed to hold Nimal Perera to account for receiving $ 800,000 into a bank account owned by him.\n\nHowever, the Sri Lankan investigations during the Gotabaya Rajapaksa regime in 2020 led nowhere. Kapila Chandrasena and his wife were briefly arrested and, upon released bail, Ms. Wijenayake fled to Australia.\n\nThe Airbus deal negotiated in 2013 by the Rajapaksa administration was subsequently terminated by its successor, Yahapalanaya, in 2015. Sri Lanka paid a cancellation penalty of around $115 million to Airbus and aircraft lessors to terminate the contract. It was also alleged that Sri Lankan also overpaid by roughly US$ 50 million above the market price when it acquired two older A330S under the same contract\u2014 which is now also embroiled in the bribery payment.\n\nThe regime protection and impunity extended to wheeler-dealers and corrupt regime operators were a way of life in Sri Lankan politics; it has, for the first time, faced a decisive assault. The de facto bipartisan consensus defending regime holders, save minor political sideshows, had been dutifully followed by both the UNP and the Rajapaksa regime. While the JVP/NPP administration\u2019s sincerity in combating corruption, or for that matter corruption under its predecessors, is yet to be determined, in this particular incident it carries no political baggage. Stakes may be different if the government chose to pursue the VFS global Visa scam.( The government, neither otherwise activist CIABOC had chosen not to, nor has the opposition SJB, true to the old bipartisan consensus.)"
