    "md5": "f57890adac3b7802ad6cff93eb38b5a3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "LAWASIA raises serious concerns over procedure in Supreme Court hearings on 22nd Amendment\n\nColombo, Sep. 16 (Daily Mirror) - The Law Association for Asia and the Pacific (LAWASIA) has expressed serious concern over the procedure followed by the Supreme Court of Sri Lanka in the constitutional challenges to the proposed 22nd Amendment to the Constitution, which seeks to extend the retirement ages of Judges of the Supreme Court and the Court of Appeal.\n\nIn a statement issued dated September 16, 2026, LAWASIA reiterated its longstanding commitment to the rule of law, judicial independence and the proper administration of justice. The organisation referred to its earlier statement of 30 June 2026, in which it had already flagged concerns about the implications of the ad hoc reform for judicial independence and the need for transparency, consultation and stakeholder engagement.\n\nLAWASIA observed that it had taken cognisance of the Commonwealth Lawyers Association\u2019s (CLA) report dated 7 September 2026, prepared after the CLA President visited Sri Lanka to observe the Supreme Court proceedings.\n\nThe LAWASIA stressed that its concerns relate solely to the procedure adopted in the hearings and that it does not express any view on the merits of the constitutional challenges or the substantive arguments advanced by the parties.\n\nThe association observed that applications seeking the recusal of the Chief Justice and requesting that the petitions be heard by a Full Court comprising all sitting Judges of the Supreme Court were declined. According to the CLA report, no reasons were provided for the dismissal of these applications other than that the Court had jurisdiction to hear the matter. The recusal application had been made on the basis that the Chief Justice would be the first Supreme Court Judge who could benefit from the proposed amendment."
  },
