    "md5": "38097c2f225ec6f4bc631186da854351",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "The association observed that applications seeking the recusal of the Chief Justice and requesting that the petitions be heard by a Full Court comprising all sitting Judges of the Supreme Court were declined. According to the CLA report, no reasons were provided for the dismissal of these applications other than that the Court had jurisdiction to hear the matter. The recusal application had been made on the basis that the Chief Justice would be the first Supreme Court Judge who could benefit from the proposed amendment.\n\nWhile acknowledging that seniority is not, of itself, determinative of the constitution of a Bench and without questioning the competence of the Judges who sat, LAWASIA stated that in proceedings of such exceptional constitutional significance, the basis upon which a Bench is constituted \u201cshould be transparent and discernible and capable of maintaining public confidence in the institutional impartiality of the Court.\u201d\n\nA further serious concern related to procedural fairness. After the oral submissions of the Interveners and the State, the Petitioners were not afforded an opportunity to make oral submissions in reply, although they were permitted to tender written replies by 3.00 pm the following day. LAWASIA observed that the opportunity to respond meaningfully to material submissions made against a party\u2019s case is an important aspect of procedural fairness.\n\n\u201cScrutiny of judicial procedure should not be regarded as an attack upon judicial independence. On the contrary, judicial independence must be safeguarded both in fact and in public perception, and procedural fairness, impartiality and transparency are essential to maintaining public confidence in the Judiciary,\u201d the statement said.\n\nThe statement was issued under the signature of TL Yap, President of LAWASIA."
  },
