    "md5": "c0bfa905246e9a903ded1c0f8fc616ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "As strategic advisers, we will create new value by providing the indispensable insights and knowledge that will enable our organisations to seize new market opportunities, pre-solve major problems and flourish in a world that will be defined by the transition to a more sustainable economy and advances in artificial intelligence (AI). So how can we evolve from where we are now to being the trusted strategic advisers of the future?\n\nBe able to tell the human stories behind the data. Finance and accounting have long been pigeon-holed as a \u2018number-crunching\u2019 profession. Sure, we are highly skilled at gathering, managing and presenting data. But it is how we interpret data that will matter in the age of AI. We must be able to understand not only what the data means for our own business, but what it means for our industry, our community and society at large. What are the risks it reveals? And the opportunities? How can we use it to create value? We must use data to look forwards as well as backwards and make insightful predictions about how markets will evolve.\n\nHave agency and continuously upskill. Becoming a trusted strategic adviser isn\u2019t something that will happen by magic. We must make it happen by investing in our own development. That means learning and enhancing the skills that will enable us to create value in future \u2013 skills such as strategic and critical thinking, collaboration and relationship building, and the interpretation of AI output. It should also go without saying that we will need to be accomplished users of AI tools, with the ability to undertake in-depth scenario planning.\n\nThe shift towards becoming a trusted strategic adviser is one of the key themes identified by Rise2040, a profession-wide research initiative launched by AICPA and CIMA. The initiative brings together 6,000 insights from across the globe to collectively explore, challenge and shape the future of public accounting and finance."
  },
