    "md5": "2f3e85505cdab57a9a0be09be024b404",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "48-hour railway strike begins\n\nColombo, September 17 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka\u2019s railway trade unions launched a 48-hour token strike at midnight yesterday (16), after talks with the Government failed to resolve their demand for the removal of Railway General Manager Ravindra Pathmapriya.\n\nTransport, Highways and Urban Development Minister Bimal Rathnayake has said he cannot agree to the unions\u2019 demand, leaving the main dispute unresolved ahead of the planned strike.\n\nThe industrial action, involving a large number of railway trade unions, is expected to disrupt train services across the country. The unions say they have lost confidence in the administration of the current General Manager and are calling for his immediate removal.\n\nThe unions had initially announced the strike based on two main demands: the removal of the Railway General Manager and the withdrawal of a letter seeking an explanation from Sri Lanka Railway Station Masters\u2019 Union General Secretary Kasun Chamara Jayasekara over comments he made to the media regarding problems affecting railway services.\n\nFollowing discussions with the Transport Ministry on September 14, the unions decided to proceed with the strike after no acceptable decision was reached on the removal of the General Manager.\n\nDuring the meeting, union representatives raised concerns over a range of administrative and operational issues within Sri Lanka Railways, employee-related matters and disruptions affecting passengers.\n\nThey told the Ministry that their confidence in the present administration had completely eroded and called for the General Manager to be removed immediately.\n\nThe unions said the dispute had remained unresolved for nearly two years and rejected a request from the Ministry Secretary for additional time to reach a final decision.\n\nThey said that the strike was not aimed at opposing the Government but at rebuilding the Railway Department, improving operational efficiency and restoring a reliable service for passengers."
  },
