    "md5": "678a222146da751eb101d5e3cdeec667",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1549,
    "chunk_text": "Justice De Silva observed that although the independence of the judiciary was a foundational constitutional principle, judicial officers remained subject to taxation imposed by law where such taxation was of general application, non-discriminatory and not specifically directed at judges or the judiciary.\n\nThe appellants had argued that judicial officers could not be regarded as employees for the purposes of the Inland Revenue Act because they did not serve under an employer-employee relationship. They contended that treating judges as employees would be inconsistent with the constitutional principle of judicial independence.\n\nThey further argued that the remuneration of judicial officers was constitutionally protected from reduction during their tenure and that the deduction of APIT effectively reduced their remuneration. They sought orders quashing the decision to subject judicial officers to APIT, preventing future deductions and requiring reimbursement of monies already deducted.\n\nThe respondents, however, maintained that judicial officers were liable to income tax under the Inland Revenue Act and that APIT was merely a mechanism for collecting income tax in advance. They argued that the tax was a general, non-discriminatory tax applicable to citizens and did not undermine judicial independence.\n\nJustice Shiran Gooneratne, while agreeing with the conclusions concerning the liability of judicial officers to income tax and the operation of APIT, expressed a different view on certain aspects of the constitutional reasoning."
