    "md5": "ee5c4c1428efa59e3d80d56ef6a1df47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka needs street-smart directors, not those buried in theory: SLID Chairman\n\nSri Lanka needs better-governed organisations and directors who are street-smart, practical and not buried in theory, as a private sector whose reputation has been battered in recent times must raise its game, Sri Lanka Institute of Directors (SLID) Chairman Dinesh Weerakkody told the institute\u2019s Annual Members Meeting and 25th anniversary celebration in Colombo on Monday.\n\nHe called for strong governance and a culture of doing the right thing across corporate Sri Lanka.\n\n\u201cThe responsibility for all of us is clear: build credible institutions,\u201d Weerakkody urged.\n\n\u201cCertainly, the next 25 years will demand even more from directors. The ability to navigate through crisis and chaos,\u201d he cautioned, pointing to artificial intelligence, global conflicts and climate disasters as forces that will fundamentally reshape the way business is done.\n\nRecalling his early years as a young board director invited by founder Chairman Deshamanya Ken Balendra to serve on the SLID\u2019s inaugural committee, Weerakkody noted that having no mandated code of corporate governance and no social media at the time, the disclosure requirements were minimal and the related party transactions were rampant.\n\n\u201cLife on boards was easy. You were not expected to rock the boat too much,\u201d he recalled.\n\nThe market has since transformed. Sri Lanka had about 235 listed companies, with a market capitalisation of around 100 billion and the rupee traded at Rs.89 to the US dollar. Today, 290 companies are listed, with a market capitalisation of Rs.8 trillion, while the exchange rate stands at Rs.328.\n\nThe number of listed companies has grown by around 23 percent, while the market capitalisation has risen eightyfold in rupee terms."
  },
