    "md5": "07e6446b585917f5af75ceaf43d12979",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "New rabies law ends arbitrary killing of animals on suspicion, veterinarian says\n\nColombo, September 17 (Daily Mirror) - Animals suspected of having rabies can no longer be arbitrarily killed or shot by police merely on suspicion under Sri Lanka\u2019s newly introduced legislation on rabies control and dog registration, Veterinary Surgeon and Animal Welfare Coalition (AWC) Executive Member Dr. Chamith Nanayakkara said.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara said the new Elimination of Rabies and Registration of Dogs Bill repeals the decades-old Rabies Ordinance (Chapter 476) and introduces a system based on veterinary examination, observation and scientific intervention. The Bill was gazetted on September 7, 2026.\n\nHe said the previous legislation had given police officers powers to destroy dogs that were sick or suspected of having rabies, while provisions also allowed animals to be killed in certain circumstances based on suspicion.\n\n\u201cAnimals can no longer be killed or shot by the police simply on suspicion of having rabies,\u201d Dr. Nanayakkara said.\n\nHe explained that under the new provisions, dogs suspected of having rabies and found in public places are to be captured and transferred to designated detention and observation facilities rather than being killed immediately.\n\nAccording to Dr. Nanayakkara, the animals must then undergo periodic examinations by a government veterinary surgeon to determine whether they are infected with rabies.\n\nHe said euthanasia would only be permitted where a government veterinary surgeon confirms that an animal is infected with rabies and considers it necessary to prevent a danger to the public. The procedure must be carried out by the government veterinary surgeon or a person authorised by the veterinarian.\n\n\u201cRabies control is now governed by scientific, humane and veterinary medical protocols,\u201d he said."
  },
