    "chunk_size_bytes": 1862,
    "chunk_text": "For example, MasterChef Australia has featured Amina Elshafei and Samira El Khafir, Muslim women who wore hijabs during the competition. One of them was eliminated following a pork dish, not because of the ingredient itself, but because the execution failed. Narmadha did not use pork, produced a good dish and did not end up at the bottom.\n\nQSome viewers felt that the choices available to the contestants were not sufficiently clear. Is that something the programme would consider?\n\nPeter Kuruvita: Maybe the final cut was not clear enough that there was a choice. I feel that was an editing issue. If we all had our time again, it may have been edited differently. The episode would still have gone ahead, but perhaps two or three words would have been changed.\n\nLook at 46 minutes of a 12 hour shoot. The content captured by nine, ten or 12 cameras can produce 80 to 100 hours of footage. From that footage, the editing team has to select what goes into the final programme. I can assure everyone that our editors will be more careful about how these moments are presented in future.\n\nSavindri Perera: I was shown saying that contestants could use gloves and demonstrate a different set of skills, because that is the truth. Pork is a specific protein that takes skill to prepare and cook. If a contestant chooses to work with it, we as judges are able to see a different skill set. The concern is that people have interpreted those comments as pressure to use the ingredient, when that was not the intention.\n\nSome people interpreted the comments as being insensitive to Muslim contestants. That was not our intention. It is important to understand how a production operates and the support that is available to contestants on set.\n\nQHow does MasterChef Sri Lanka accommodate contestants with different religious, cultural, dietary and medical requirements?"
