    "md5": "1e443ec5834b91b89b08a97f2dc10337",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "Leopard cub rescued after wandering into house in Bulathkohupitiya\n\nColombo, September 17 (Daily Mirror) - A well-grown leopard cub that had wandered into a house in\u00a0Dedugala, Bulathkohupitiya, was safely rescued by wildlife officials following a coordinated operation.\n\nThe rescue operation was launched on September 15 after the Bulathkohupitiya Wildlife Office received information that a leopard (Panthera pardus) had entered a residence in the area.\n\nWildlife officers, acting on veterinary advice, rescued the animal and subsequently attempted to reunite it with its mother. However, the attempt was unsuccessful.\n\nThe leopard was therefore transported to the Randenigala Wildlife Health Management Unit on September 16 for further treatment and observation.\n\nWildlife veterinary officers and other personnel are currently attending to the animal and making arrangements to release it into a suitable habitat after it receives the necessary medical treatment.\n\nThe leopard is a strictly protected species under the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance and is considered threatened with extinction.\n\nWildlife authorities urged the public to avoid approaching or attempting to rescue leopards found trapped in snares or confined to a particular location.\n\nMembers of the public who encounter such an animal are advised to immediately move away from the area and inform the nearest wildlife office or contact the Department of Wildlife Conservation emergency hotline on 1992.\n\nWildlife officials also commended the homeowner and residents in the area for following instructions and extending their cooperation to ensure the safe rescue of the animal.\n\nThe operation was conducted under the guidance of Saman Padmasiri, Wildlife Range Officer of the Ma-imbul Kanda Range Office.\n\nWildlife Range Assistants W.A. Udith Viduranga and K.P. Udaya Karunanayake, and Field Assistants K.A.N. Kaluarachchi and D.W. Anura Jayatissa of the Bulathkohupitiya Wildlife Office participated in the operation."
  },
