    "md5": "a26fd73fff15b6dbd4c096b37c912f05",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1741,
    "chunk_text": "PM, new Chinese envoy discuss education, digital transformation push\n\nPrime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya met with newly appointed Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Wei Huaxiang at Temple Trees on\u00a0 Wednesday, with discussions centred on deepening bilateral cooperation in education and digital transformation ahead of the 70th anniversary of diplomatic relations between the two countries in 2027.\n\nAmbassador Wei presented his credentials as Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary on 25 August, 2026.\n\nA key focus of the talks was the Chinese Government\u2019s continued support for providing school uniforms to Sri Lankan students. Both sides discussed shifting from the current annual arrangement to a longer-term framework spanning three to five years, aimed at ensuring the timely and predictable delivery of uniforms ahead of each new school year.\n\nThe two sides also reviewed progress on the Chinese-assisted digital education initiative, under which 900 smart devices are expected to support 500 smart classrooms across the country as part of Sri Lanka\u2019s wider effort to digitalise its education sector.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya briefed Ambassador Wei on the Task Force on the Digital Transformation of Education, which is developing policy for digital education. Ambassador Wei welcomed the initiative and pledged China\u2019s support for the Task Force\u2019s work, with further engagement between the Task Force and the Chinese Embassy expected to follow.\n\nDiscussions further covered scholarship opportunities for Sri Lankan students under the Chinese Government Scholarship Program and the Chinese Ambassador\u2019s Scholarship Program, along with the need for stronger regional and international cooperation on children\u2019s online safety."
