    "chunk_text": "Cabinet green lights  39 hospital infrastructure projects for 2027 rollout\n\nGovt. pushes \"Arogya\"\u00a0primary care model to ease tertiary hospital overcrowding\n\nHealth Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa says Sri Lanka aims to replicate India\u2019s Ayushman wellness centre model, with e-health records and National ID-linked health numbers planned to route patients between care levels\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved the implementation of 39 new development projects aimed at upgrading infrastructure across Government health service institutions, including hospitals, as part of a broader push to address long-standing infrastructure gaps and modernise health service facilities.\n\nCabinet Spokesman and Health Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa said earlier this week the projects stem from a review of 41 proposals submitted during 2025 and 2026, evaluated by the Department of National Planning. Following that evaluation, the Department recommended the projects be initiated from 2027, and they have since been incorporated into the Public Investment Program for 2027\u20132031.\n\n\u201cOf the 41 proposals reviewed, 39 were assessed as ready for implementation, with provisions for these to be allocated through the 2027 Budget,\u201d Dr. Jayatissa said at the weekly post-Cabinet media briefing.\n\nHe said the 41 proposals focus on infrastructure development at tertiary-level institutions, including district, teaching, national and specialised hospitals.\n\nTurning to the Government\u2019s \u2018Arogya\u2019 wellness centre initiative, Dr. Jayatissa framed it as part of a decades-old global consensus on strengthening primary healthcare, tracing the approach back nearly five decades to the WHO\u2019s Alma-Ata Declaration.\n\nCiting India\u2019s Ayushman Bharat wellness centres as a reference model, he said Sri Lanka\u2019s Arogyaya centres are intended to extend coverage to hospitals that were left out even under the World Bank-funded healthcare project of 2018\u20132019, while also covering provincial hospitals and B- and C-level facilities."
  },
