    "md5": "0eac231938bf6a7d2e6a561bdf44469b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1268,
    "chunk_text": "Citing India\u2019s Ayushman Bharat wellness centres as a reference model, he said Sri Lanka\u2019s Arogyaya centres are intended to extend coverage to hospitals that were left out even under the World Bank-funded healthcare project of 2018\u20132019, while also covering provincial hospitals and B- and C-level facilities.\n\nLeveraging advances in ICT, Dr. Jayatissa said the Government hopes to issue individuals a health number linked to their National Identity Card (NIC) number, enabling the maintenance of electronic health (e-health) records and allowing patients to be directed from primary care facilities to secondary, tertiary or specialised hospitals as needed.\n\nStating that the 2026 Budget allocated Rs. 1,500 million for this initiative, he said he hoped the Government would increase that allocation in the upcoming Budget 2027.\n\n\u201cThrough this system, the Government intends to gradually reduce the patient population served per primary care centre from 10,000 to 5,000,\u201d he added.\n\nOn staffing, Dr. Jayatissa said 260 Public Health Nursing Officers (PHNOs) have already been trained, with another 300 currently undergoing training. \u201cWe also plan to recruit an additional 500 PHNOs to support the rollout of primary healthcare service centres,\u201d he added."
