    "md5": "cefb5848893ff9ebea05dc63d2837eb7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWhen a woman entrepreneur creates jobs, she is not only growing her own business. She is creating opportunities for other people and supporting families and communities.\u201d\n\nInnovation would also be considered broadly, ranging from products and services to technology adoption and new approaches to solving business and social challenges.\n\nAlongside entrepreneurship and leadership development, WIM is introducing the Respectful Workplace Audit and Certification to encourage organisations to benchmark their workplace practices against internationally aligned standards.\n\nThe initiative will assess organisations against International Finance Corporation (IFC) Respectful Workplace guidelines, with qualifying organisations receiving WIM endorsement and certification in partnership with TLM UK, an Ofqual-recognised awarding body.\n\nA TLM representative said organisations increasingly needed to view respectful workplace practices as fundamental to business sustainability rather than simply an HR policy.\n\n\u201cA respectful workplace is not merely an optional perk or a policy on a piece of paper. It is the fundamental bedrock of sustainable business, human wellbeing and long-term organisational success,\u201d the representative said.\n\nThe initiative would also give organisations a mechanism to continuously assess and improve their practices.\n\n\u201cThis program is far more than just certification. It is a formal recognition of an organisation\u2019s deep commitment to building a culture of respect, inclusion, accountability and professional excellence,\u201d the representative added.\n\nApplications close on September 30 2026 via www.wimglobalevents.com, with certification limited to 15 organisations annually.\n\nThe December program will also bring an international dimension through UK-based Sri Lankan professionals Deena Tissera and Lushani Kodituwakku, alongside local business and leadership figures chairing the three initiatives."
  },
