    "description": "Unpaid care work drives women out of jobs",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Unpaid-care-work-drives-women-out-of-jobs/108-350556",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789700326.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-18-dailymirrorlk-160d98e0-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "86fe3de8a36aa4b525c8cdef030c7491",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Unpaid care work drives women out of jobs\n\nThe Government has identified unpaid and unequal 'care responsibilities' as a major reason why women leave the workforce, saying families and the economy cannot reach their full potential unless care work is recognised as a shared responsibility.\n\nDeputy Minister of Women and Child Affairs said the issue was particularly important in Sri Lanka, where women continue to face difficulties balancing employment with childcare, caring for elderly family members, household duties and other unpaid care work.\n\n\u201cWhen we ask why women leave the workforce, why families struggle to balance work and home, or why opportunities remain unequal, we often arrive at the same answer: care,\u201d the Deputy Minister said.\n\nAddressing a regional gathering in Seoul on transforming care systems for women's economic empowerment, she said care work was often ignored and undervalued, despite being essential to families, workplaces and economies.\n\n\u201cEvery success story is also a care story,\u201d she said, noting that behind every child who learns, every worker who contributes and every leader who serves, there is often someone providing care.\n\nThe Deputy Minister said care should no longer be seen mainly as a responsibility for women, but as a shared responsibility of families, communities, employers and the State.\n\nShe said Sri Lanka was taking steps to develop a more inclusive and effective social care system, with the National Social Protection Strategy identifying social care as a priority.\n\nA National Action Plan on Social Care has also been developed and is awaiting Ministerial approval, she said.\n\nThe Government's long-term goal is to make care an important part of national plans, programmes and budgets, while reducing the burden on families and improving women's access to employment and economic opportunities."
