    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f5bfd2f662b36ed85e8f64baec4d764e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "He also criticised the expenditure on post-exposure treatment for people bitten by animals, saying nearly Rs. 1 billion in public funds was being spent on such treatment without sufficiently involving the nearly 700 DAPH veterinary surgeons stationed at regional veterinary offices to address the source of the problem.\n\nHe argued that the Health Ministry lacked an adequate mechanism to procure and distribute vaccines, maintain cold chains, identify high-risk areas and provide emergency veterinary responses required for a comprehensive animal rabies control programme.\n\nHe said the proposed Bill\u2019s requirement for veterinary supervision of vaccination programmes was intended to ensure the quality of vaccination and animal welfare.\n\n\u201cHaving a veterinarian available in rare but possible cases of complications following vaccination also ensures the well-being of animals,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Bill was gazetted on September 7 and seeks to establish a new legal framework covering rabies vaccination, dog registration, dog population control and commercial dog breeding. Cabinet had earlier approved the preparation and publication of the legislation.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara also disputed claims that the rabies elimination programme previously handed over to the DAPH in 2018 had to be taken back by the Health Ministry because of a lack of progress.\n\nHe described that account as a \u201chalf-truth\u201d, claiming that the programme had been handed over without adequate arrangements and was reclaimed within six months.\n\nHe further alleged that the Health Ministry had not updated its database with vaccination and sterilisation figures from that period despite repeated reminders, while continuing to attribute shortcomings to the DAPH.\n\nAt the same time, Dr. Nanayakkara acknowledged the contribution of PHIs to rabies control at grassroots level."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
