    "md5": "bc35f820c1c11696b4fbbacbb8fe9d4b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2015,
    "chunk_text": "Pilots carry Air Lanka legacy into next generation\n\nColombo, September 18 (Daily Mirror) - The legacy of Sri Lanka\u2019s National Carrier is being carried into a new generation of pilots, with two SriLankan Airlines flight crew members sharing their experiences at a special aviation talk organised to mark the airline\u2019s 47th anniversary.\n\nCaptain Shavantha Pedris and Senior First Officer Nuwan Jayawickrama took part in the sixth episode of The Skyline Stories, an aviation talk series organised by SriLankan Aviation College.\n\nThe session provided a unique connection between the early years of Air Lanka, established in 1979, and the present-day SriLankan Airlines, as both pilots are sons of members of the airline\u2019s pioneering flight crew.\n\nCaptain Shavantha Pedris is the son of Captain Ranjit Pedris, while Senior First Officer Nuwan Jayawickrama is the son of Captain Nihal Jayawickrama.\n\nSharing his experience, Captain Pedris said growing up with a father involved in aviation had a strong influence on his decision to pursue a career in the industry.\n\nHe said his father inspired him from a young age and encouraged him to aim higher.\n\n\u201cEach experience has shaped who I am and strengthened my passion for aviation. Now, I feel it is my turn to give back to the industry that has given me so much,\u201d he said.\n\nSenior First Officer Jayawickrama said aviation had always been part of his family life and becoming a pilot therefore felt like a natural possibility.\n\nHowever, he said following in the footsteps of a successful parent also came with expectations.\n\n\u201cThe goal is not simply to follow someone else\u2019s journey, but to learn from it and build your own,\u201d he said.\n\nThe discussion highlighted the experiences of the pioneering generation that helped establish Air Lanka and the challenges faced by today's aviation professionals.\n\nThe session also reflected on the role played by the National Carrier in the development of Sri Lanka\u2019s aviation and tourism sectors over the past 47 years."
