    "md5": "7f3bbd826e5d5748d7e3ba43d0e1dca7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. eyes economic transformation as IMF reviews progress\n\nPresident\u00a0 Dissanayake in discussion with a delegation led by IMF Mission Chief Evan Papageorgiou at the Presidential Secretariat yesterday\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake yesterday said the government would seek to convert Sri Lanka\u2019s hard-won economic stability into stronger growth and improved living standards, as the International Monetary Fund (IMF) assessed the country\u2019s progress under its US$ 3 billion bailout programme.\n\nMeeting an IMF delegation led by Mission Chief Evan Papageorgiou at the Presidential Secretariat, the President said recent indicators showed that the economy had moved towards stability, providing a platform for a broader transformation.\n\n\u201cOur objective is to use this stability as a strong foundation to take the country towards a transformative era,\u201d the President was quoted as saying.\n\nHe stressed that the gains from the recovery must translate into tangible improvements in household living standards, as higher energy costs and other pressures continue to weigh on the public.\n\nThe IMF is in the island nation to conduct the seventh review of Sri Lanka\u2019s Extended Fund Facility (EFF) and its 2026 Article IV Consultation. The mission, which began on September 10, is scheduled to conclude on September 23.\n\nPapageorgiou acknowledged Sri Lanka\u2019s economic progress, including its fiscal discipline, stronger government revenue collection and efforts to attract foreign investment, according to the President\u2019s Media Division.\n\nHe also underscored the need to preserve the stability achieved so far as the country moves into 2027 and beyond.\n\nThe President said the IMF-supported programme had provided an important foundation for restoring stability following Sri Lanka\u2019s worst economic crisis in decades. He added that the improved economic position had strengthened the country\u2019s ability to absorb the fallout from Cyclone Ditwah and the conflict in the Middle East."
  },
