    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e916b367662a34050378a9f6e2f6143a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1702,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe government is committed to sorting out whatever differences that are there to give and take and come to that agreement so that there is some guarantee that can be given to domestic exporters that that market opportunity is available not only today but moving forward,\u201d he added.\n\n\u201cIt was a do-or-die effort on the part of Sri Lanka to ensure that we get a good deal out of the negotiations that we undertook immediately after getting one of the highest tariff rates back in April 2025,\u201d he recalled.\n\n\u201cWe were able, as a result of skillful negotiations, reaching out, getting the right information at the right time, and acting upon it immediately, to bring down the tariff rate from the initial 44 percent to 33 percent, then subsequently to 20 percent, and now at a favourable rate of 10 percent.\u201d\n\nHe stressed that this was not the end of the process, pointing out that the 27-member European Union had already finalised its agreement as a bloc, while approximately 50 countries had signed agreements, with some already implementing them.\n\nThe baseline requirement in these negotiations was clear, according to Samarasinghe. Every country that finally agreed to sign had to grant either complete or near-complete duty-free access for American exports into its market. Many signatories gave complete access, while some introduced time-bound plans that would eventually lead to complete duty-free access.\n\nTurning to the link between tariffs and geopolitics, he observed that the general consensus was that the US tariff regime was shaped not only by domestic compulsions, such as revenue generation and helping American manufacturers compete, but also by a carrot-and-stick approach."
  },
  {
