    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "Many countries secured favourable rates by committing to major purchases and investments in the US. One East Asian country, likely Japan, agreed to buy US$ 8 billion worth of US agricultural products and, for the first time in its history, allowed US rice imports. Another, likely South Korea, agreed to invest billions of dollars in revitalising the US shipbuilding industry, while a third, likely Taiwan, agreed to shift semiconductor production to the US.\n\n\u201cThese are the kinds of deals that were used to leverage the market access that the US was able to give countries that all desperately sought to get. Sri Lanka was no different,\u201d he remarked, adding that Sri Lanka had secured one of the best deals from the negotiations despite not yet signing the agreement.\n\nWhen he left Washington, bilateral relations between Sri Lanka and the US were at a high point, Samarasinghe noted. Describing the US as a true friend of Sri Lanka, he cited the four Coast Guard cutters and 10 helicopters provided to the country, support through the US International Development Finance Corporation (DFC) and assistance following the tsunami.\n\n\u201cAll this has been 100 percent grants and not loans that Sri Lanka has to pay back,\u201d he said.\n\nHe also credited the US with helping Sri Lanka secure its IMF programme, supporting the country during debt restructuring discussions with the Paris Club and assisting in the ongoing Hamilton Reserve Bank case. The US-based bondholder sued Sri Lanka in a New York court in 2022 over its holdings of defaulted International Sovereign Bonds.\n\n\u201cWhen it came to the IMF negotiations, I was there in the thick of it, and without US help, Sri Lanka would never have got the IMF deal. The US is a true friend of Sri Lanka, and if we have them on board, their cooperation makes it that much easier for us,\u201d he stated.\n\nSamarasinghe expressed hope that his successor would not only maintain the relationship but consolidate it further."
  },
