    "md5": "18364000c5c039e6f7d1cf5b0f5255da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "It had been found in the forest by a local resident, who initially thought it was a domestic kitten. As it grew older, however, it became clear that it did not behave like a house cat.\n\nDr Nogales-Ascarrunz had heard of other sightings of small wild cats in the dense forests of the country's Yungas region, but these had not been investigated by scientists.\n\n\"There's so few records or pictures, no individual in a museum, so it was hiding because nobody was looking,\" said Eduardo Eizirik, a conservation geneticist at the Pontifical Catholic University of Rio Grande do Sul in Brazil.\n\nThe study, published in Current Biology, uncovered further surprises, suggesting there are five rather than three tiger cat species, including the newly described Tilcaya cat (Leopardus tilcayo) of Bolivia.\n\nCo-researcher Dr Caroline Sartor, of the Wildlife Conservation Research Unit at the University of Oxford, said the discovery was important for conservation because it is impossible to protect a species if we don't know what it is or where it lives.\n\n\"These are animals similar in size to a domestic cat, so you might think we would know them all by now, but this discovery shows how much biodiversity is still to be uncovered in some of the less-studied regions of the world,\" she said.\n\nTiger cat species are considered vulnerable to extinction because of deforestation, habitat loss and illegal hunting, although the new species is missing from previous assessments.\n\nThe discovery could also have wider implications for the world's cat family.\n\nBoth wild and domestic cats belong to the biological family Felidae, which includes every living cat species from tiny house pets to massive tigers.\n\nThere are officially 41 species recognised but the new discovery, and the finding that there may be several species of tiger cat, brings the number to 44 or more."
