    "chunk_text": "He further said the CID had forwarded the extracts of its investigations relating to the incident to the Attorney General.\n\nHe added that under Section 69 of the Anti-Corruption Act, CIABOC has the statutory power to seek the Attorney General\u2019s advice regarding investigations.\n\nHe said all investigation files would be studied and necessary legal action would be taken as soon as possible.\n\nThe Deputy Solicitor General also said the suspect had been produced before court under Section 149 of the Anti-Corruption Act, accompanied by a certificate issued by the Director General of CIABOC.\n\nHe submitted that where such a certificate issued by the Director General of CIABOC had been produced, bail could be granted only if exceptional circumstances were established.\n\nThe Chief Magistrate then inquired from the Deputy Solicitor General about the action taken regarding the warrants issued by court against the third and fourth suspects in the case.\n\nIn response, Deputy Solicitor General Bandara said steps had been taken through Interpol to obtain Red Notices in respect of the warrant issued against the third suspect, Shashindra Rajapaksa, who is said to be in the United States.\n\nHe also informed court that steps were being taken to obtain a Red Notice in respect of the second suspect, Priyanka Neomali Wijenayake, who is said to have fled to Australia.\n\nHowever, the Deputy Solicitor General said indictments would have to be filed against the suspects in order to expedite these proceedings.\n\nMaking submissions, President\u2019s Counsel Shavindra Fernando said an international investigation had been conducted into the Airbus transaction and that the suspect\u2019s name did not appear in any of the relevant reports.\n\nHe also said the suspect\u2019s name was not mentioned in the report of the commission appointed by former President Maithripala Sirisena to investigate the matter."
