    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIf no one accepted the responsibility of running the country during the \u2018Aragalaya\u2019 in 2022, the future of the nation would have been bleak. We knew that the country could be re-stabilised. Therefore, we took over the responsibility without fear,\u201d he also said in response to a question about his thoughts on the decision to take over the nation back in 2022.\n\nHe said, \u201cI introduced a white paper to reform education. Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) launched protests in schools and universities. Late President JR called me and said not to do anything about it. I asked him as to what I should do with the white paper. Then Mr Jayewardene said you can do anything in three months. You could learn patience from JR. He was 70 years old when he became the President. Then you have to learn from your experience and the experience of others. Also, one has to be committed. One cannot achieve victory without defeat. One cannot hold on to victory forever. One has to let go of power and go to opposition in politics. President Jayewardene also told us to be prepared to go to opposition one day. One has to taste defeat as well. There were no mobile phones, no land phones and no vehicles during the time I became a cabinet minister. Owning a cycle was a big thing. Youth were stuck in villages. They had employment opportunities only within the villages. However, the world changed. Economy was developed, and people ended up with money in their wallets. The tourism industry connected Sri Lanka to the world. I did not grow up in such a developed society. We could own a landline phone, as I was living in an urban area during my youth. I had knowledge of the world. However, other young people did not have any expectations during that time. Many dropped out of school after Grade eight. One had job opportunities only in state-owned companies. The biggest state-owned company was the Tobacco Company during my youth. Everything changed during President Jayewardene\u2019s time."
