    "md5": "d5176cf545acc7b80924db263c01489a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1727,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cSo, there is clearly a huge amount of curiosity and interest among the businesses in ensuring that they are not left behind and that they too move towards becoming Agentic Enterprises,\u201d she observed.\n\nThe company also detailed Koa, a CRM reasoning model built with NVIDIA Nemotron technology and an Enterprise AI Harness that wraps large language models in governed data, guardrails and permission controls.\n\nMeanwhile, Salesforce South Asia Vice President of Solution Engineering Gavin Barfield argued that the consumable, out-of-the-box agents would help prevent an AI divide from opening between large corporates and smaller firms without sizeable IT budgets.\n\n\u201cIt is important that the SMEs are able to take advantage of this technology in exactly the same way that the larger enterprises can,\u201d he told the briefing.\n\nHowever, Barfield acknowledged that the fragmented legacy estates remain a barrier to scaled deployment, contending that AI cannot perform effectively on inconsistent data dispersed across multiple systems. In his view, consolidating that data into a trusted, governed layer through Data 360 and Informatica, which Salesforce acquired partly for this purpose, is the critical first step.\n\nBhattacharya stressed that the company does not favour a rip-and-replace approach.\n\n\u201cWe try to ensure that whatever investments a company has already made, whether in a data layer or other applications, can be used and brought into the architecture alongside Salesforce,\u201d she explained, noting that Data 360\u2019s zero-copy architecture allows intelligence to be applied to data residing in the existing lakehouses without replicating it.\n\n81% of Sri Lankans Now Use AI Instead of Google \u2014 Is That Safe?"
