    "md5": "c52c464c954e5039f46dae5cee9a7be4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka connects first grid-scale battery storage facility\n\nDeveloped by Vidullanka PLC and WindForce PLC, through project company StoreX Ltd\n\nIt is first of 13 battery energy storage system projects planned across country\n\nSri Lanka yesterday connected its first grid-scale standalone battery energy storage system (BESS) to the national grid, marking a key step towards storing the surplus solar power and reducing the dependence on the costly fossil fuel-based generation during the peak demand hours.\n\nThe 10 MW/40 MWh facility in Anuradhapura was commissioned under the patronage of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake.\n\nDeveloped by Vidullanka PLC and WindForce PLC, through project company StoreX Ltd, the facility is the first of 13 BESS projects planned across the country, with a combined capacity of 130 MW/520 MWh.\n\nOf the total, 12 facilities are being developed under StoreX, while the partners are developing the 13th project in Vavunathivu through Solar Universe Ltd.\n\nThe remaining facilities are expected to be connected to the national grid within the next two to three months.\n\nThe battery storage systems capture the excess solar electricity generated during the daytime and release it to the grid during the periods of higher demand at night, helping balance the supply, improve grid stability and reduce the use of fossil fuel-based power.\n\nEnergy Minister Anura Karunathilake said Sri Lanka currently generates close to 3,000 MW of solar power during the day, compared with the average daytime electricity demand of about 2,800 MW.\n\nThe night-time demand, however, rises to around 3,100 MW and could increase further by February, under the forecast El Ni\u00f1o conditions.\n\n\u201cOur challenge has been that, until now, we have not had the capacity to store the excess solar power generated during the daytime,\u201d Karunathilake said.\n\nThe government is implementing battery storage projects with a combined capacity of 160 MW, including 130 MW being developed by WindForce and its partners."
  },
