    "chunk_text": "\u201cOur challenge has been that, until now, we have not had the capacity to store the excess solar power generated during the daytime,\u201d Karunathilake said.\n\nThe government is implementing battery storage projects with a combined capacity of 160 MW, including 130 MW being developed by WindForce and its partners.\n\nThe authorities have also initiated procurement for several additional battery storage projects, including facilities with capacities of 400 MW, 250 MW and 150 MW. Some are under evaluation and are expected to enter the system by the first half of 2027.\n\nKarunathilake said expanding the storage capacity would allow the grid to absorb more renewable energy while reducing the high cost of fossil fuel-based electricity generation at night.\n\nThe Anuradhapura project also marks Vidullanka\u2019s entry into grid-scale energy storage, expanding its operations beyond electricity generation.\n\nThe company is preparing to commission its eighth ground-mounted solar facility in Hambantota, which will raise its total installed capacity to 96 MW across 27 projects."
