    "md5": "0cfa8c0c45a7f0bafe1125528c453b5f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1923,
    "chunk_text": "Cricket lies dying as politics and cash rule the roost\n\nThe cricketing season is with us. Our men\u2019s and women\u2019s teams are doing battle with the best and weakest cricket playing nations. Our men\u2019s team is being mercilessly thumped in England. The Lankan lasses are doing much better,\u00a0 though without the fanfare their male counterparts receive.\n\nMost of us Lankans -- thanks to the indebtedness of our country -- cannot afford to follow our teams as they travel to diverse cricketing venues in different parts of the world. But, thanks to our government\u2019s efforts in sending our young people to different parts of the world in search of employment, it has ensured in our cricketers receiving a degree of support as they match skills with their international counterparts.\n\nCricket in its pristine form was about the spirit of sportsmanship, fairness and respect of law. It was above the final runs on the board. It was not about winning or losing, but how you played the game. But as Bob Dylan sang not so long ago, \u2018The Times They Are-a Changing\u2019.\n\nCricket like all things in this world is also changing. Sometimes for the better, but occasionally things get worse before they get better. For instance during a bygone era, the \u2018umpire\u2019s word in cricketing parlance \u2018was law\u2019. He/she \u2013 the umpire -- was on-field and none questioned his decision.\n\nThat law now has now been turned on its head. The on-field umpire\u2019s word is no longer law. Players often question his decisions and are referred to a third umpire sitting alone with machines to aid him. He verifies the on-field umpire\u2019s decision and delivers his verdict.\n\nWhile the third umpire decisions ensures exactitude, acceptance of an umpire\u2019s decision was an acceptance that an umpire could be wrong, but his/her mistake was due to human error and had to be accepted as such. It also ensured the respect due to the position of the umpire."
  },
