    "language": "en",
    "md5": "29419a61072d3028d24765857c7e8df4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "While the third umpire decisions ensures exactitude, acceptance of an umpire\u2019s decision was an acceptance that an umpire could be wrong, but his/her mistake was due to human error and had to be accepted as such. It also ensured the respect due to the position of the umpire.\n\nAt the time India was angered at a terror attack carried out allegedly by a Pakistani group at Pahalgam. If the Indian cricketing authorities wanted to protest the attack, they could have protested Pakistan\u2019s participation in the tournament or refused to play against that team. By refusing to shake hands,\u00a0 they dragged India\u2019s national politics into the game.\n\nAccording to the \u2018Indian Express\u2019, reports made it clear the act (refusing to shake hands) was \u201c\u2026premeditated and carefully choreographed. At the toss both captains skipped the usual pre-match handshake \u2013 a directive reportedly relayed by the match referee to Pakistan in advance.\n\nDuring the recently concluded Women\u2019s Asia Cup tournament, the victorious Indian women\u2019s cricket team refused to accept the trophy from the president of the Asia Cup tournament because he was also a Pakistani Cabinet Minister.\n\nYet, the ICC took no corrective action against the BCCI. However, when the Lankan government took steps to stamp out corruption in Sri Lanka Board of Cricket, the ICC immediately took away Lanka\u2019s hosting rights to the inaugural Women\u2019s Champions Trophy (2027) claiming government interference in cricket administration!\n\nIn February this year, there was another controversy as the Bangladeshi Cricket Board (BCB)\u00a0 requested their T20 World Cup Tournament games scheduled to be played in India be shifted to Sri Lanka\u00a0 citing security concerns due to growing tensions between the countries.\n\nICC rejected the request. After being given a final deadline to play in India, the BCB refused to travel, and\u00a0 ICC\u00a0 replaced Bangladesh with Scotland.\u00a0 Bangladesh felt poorly treated."
  },
