    "chunk_text": "Such battlefield surprises neither the US nor Israel seemed to have taken into consideration when they rushed to attack Iran and triggered a war without giving much thought to adverse global ramifications that could ensue. Oil prices, particularly diesel, have been skyrocketing since the fall of Bab al\u2011Mandab, or the Gate of Tears. True to its meaning, the whole world wails over rising prices.\n\nAmid the post\u2011World War I geopolitical shake\u2011up after the Ottomans lost their Middle Eastern territories, the newly established Wahhabi\u2011led Saudi Arabia, backed by Britain, launched a war on the revived Zaydi imamate in 1934. The brief war ended with the Yemeni imamate conceding three northern provinces, which today form part of Saudi Arabia\u2019s southern region. In an interesting twist, Saudi Arabia backed the Zaydi imamate when a civil war broke out in 1962, after pro\u2011Nasserite military officers captured power and dissolved the thousand\u2011year\u2011old imamate. To support the new republic, Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser sent 70,000 troops. Some 26,000 Egyptians died in the civil war. Egypt\u2019s involvement in Yemen\u2014dubbed Nasser\u2019s Vietnam\u2014was a key reason for its defeat against Israel in the 1967 war.\n\nMeanwhile, Britain which was occupying Aden in the south left in 1967 following an uprising by left\u2011inclined youth. Yemen remained divided along the Cold War lines\u2014North Yemen was supported by Saudi Arabia and the West, and the South by the Soviet Union. The end of the Cold War led to Yemeni unification, but the power\u2011sharing arrangement in favour of the densely populated North\u2014with North\u2019s president Ali Abdullah Saleh as head of state\u2014gave rise to regular disputes and even civil wars. Al\u2011Qaeda elements found safe haven in the south and fished in Yemen\u2019s troubled waters. In October 2000, they carried out a suicide attack on a US warship near Aden, 11 months before 9/11, killing 17 marines. In response, US carried out regular airstrikes on Al-Qaeda targets."
