    "md5": "e7145b6b2b7a668b98e25a62d518e21c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, Britain which was occupying Aden in the south left in 1967 following an uprising by left\u2011inclined youth. Yemen remained divided along the Cold War lines\u2014North Yemen was supported by Saudi Arabia and the West, and the South by the Soviet Union. The end of the Cold War led to Yemeni unification, but the power\u2011sharing arrangement in favour of the densely populated North\u2014with North\u2019s president Ali Abdullah Saleh as head of state\u2014gave rise to regular disputes and even civil wars. Al\u2011Qaeda elements found safe haven in the south and fished in Yemen\u2019s troubled waters. In October 2000, they carried out a suicide attack on a US warship near Aden, 11 months before 9/11, killing 17 marines. In response, US carried out regular airstrikes on Al-Qaeda targets.\n\nSaleh and the Houthis parted ways in 2017 when he signed a deal with Saudi Arabia and the UAE. Months later, Saleh was killed by the Houthis. The ensuing war plunged Yemen into what the UN described as the world\u2019s worst humanitarian crisis, with thousands of children dying from hunger and diseases such as cholera. In 2022, a UN\u2011brokered ceasefire between Saudi Arabia and the Houthis took effect in Yemen.\n\nIn the meantime, in December last year, Saudi Arabia bombed two UAE ships supplying weapons to the southern separatists, which were on the verge of capturing two provinces from Saudi\u2011backed government forces. The UAE withdrew under Saudi threats.\n\nThe ceasefire collapsed in July when Saudi\u2011backed Yemeni government forces bombed the Sanaa airport, preventing an Iranian civilian aircraft from bringing home a top Houthi delegation, after they attended the funeral of Iranian leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. The Houthis hit back at Saudi Arabia. The war broke out. Nations panicked when the Houthis, in a lightning advance, captured Bab al\u2011Mandab and continued to gain battlefield victories."
