    "md5": "3ad61968672df7a3e3ed1bfaf9d0bed4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1847,
    "chunk_text": "Knox in his book described the situation vividly:\u00a0 \u201cGreat vexation they gave the King by their irruptions into his Dominions. Great mischief they did him, the often times great loss on their sides. Great battles have been lost and won between them with great destruction of men on both parts. But being greatly distressed, at last he (Rajasinghe) sent and called in the Hollanders to his aid.\u201d\n\nHistorian K. M. de Silva in \u201cA History of Sri Lanka\u201d explained as to how the situation developed to a point where Rajasinghe\u2019s sole objective became the expulsion of the Portuguese. To achieve this goal,\u00a0 he was willing to pay a heavy price. K. M. de Silva wrote: The Portuguese, confined to the West Coast since they landed in 1505, had gradually strengthened their position. The conquest of the Jaffnapatan gave them to control over the lucrative pearl fisheries and the island\u2019s elephant trade. Jaffna at the time, was the main market for elephants captured in the island.\n\nWith the interest to expand, the Portugese had been over the years attempting to make intrusions to the Kandyan Kingdom. Their attempt to install Dona Catherina as the reigning Monarch was frustrated by Konnappu Bandara who\u00a0 married\u00a0 her and secured his claim to the Kandyan Kingdom. Having installed himself as Vimaladharmasuriya I, (1591-1604CE), he strengthened the Kandyan Kingdom defeating Portugese hopes of conquering Kandy.\n\nSenarath (1604-1635CE), his successor,\u00a0 continued Vimaladharmasuriya\u2019s policy of being anti-Portugese. Nevertheless, in order to prevent Portugese intrusions to the Kandyan Kingdom, Senarath signed a treaty with the Portugese in 1617 and acknowledged their authority over the maritime provinces. According to K.M. de Silva, Senarath even agreed to pay tribute to the Portugese with a promise to deny entry to their enemies."
