    "chunk_text": "Senarath (1604-1635CE), his successor,\u00a0 continued Vimaladharmasuriya\u2019s policy of being anti-Portugese. Nevertheless, in order to prevent Portugese intrusions to the Kandyan Kingdom, Senarath signed a treaty with the Portugese in 1617 and acknowledged their authority over the maritime provinces. According to K.M. de Silva, Senarath even agreed to pay tribute to the Portugese with a promise to deny entry to their enemies.\n\nIt was with the ascension of Rajasinghe II that signs of change in the Kandyan policy towards the Portugese appeared. Having resorted to a more aggressive policy, he conducted intrusions deep into Portugese held areas with the aim of driving them out of the island.\n\nIn the first Treaty signed in 1633 with the Portuguese however, Rajasinghe recognised Portugese control over the eastern ports. His mood appeared to have changed as the King, adopting a more aggressive policy, sought foreign assistance to drive out the Portugese. This led him to commence negotiations with the Dutch which culminated in the Treaty of 1638.\n\nThe Dutch rapidly captured Trincomalee and Batticaloa in 1639 from the Portuguese and handed them over to King Rajasinghe. However, when the Dutch won over Galle and Negombo (in 1640) they refused to transfer control claiming that the Kandyan ruler had not paid the agreed military expenses for the previous expedition. As historian K. M. de Silva notes,\u00a0 \u00a0the eastern ports were not major cinnamon producing areas,\u00a0 whereas Galle and Negombo commanded access to some of the richest cinnamon lands on the island.\n\nIn 1656, the Portugese Fort in Colombo surrendered,\u00a0 but it took two more years to eliminate them completely,\u00a0 ending over 150 years of Portugese presence in the island. The last to capitulate was the Jaffna Fort."
  },
