    "md5": "ef01c3a675f5865da1c7706d5efcaa02",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1662,
    "chunk_text": "In 1656, the Portugese Fort in Colombo surrendered,\u00a0 but it took two more years to eliminate them completely,\u00a0 ending over 150 years of Portugese presence in the island. The last to capitulate was the Jaffna Fort.\n\nWhile the English refused to concede to Dutch claims of the trade monopoly, their attempt to gain entry on the east coast of Sri Lanka made the Dutch rethink of the strategic importance of the eastern ports -- at least Trincomalee and, if permitted, Batticaloa.\n\nFortunately for the Dutch, according to K. M. De Silva, a rebellion against Rajasinghe in 1665 compelled him to seek Dutch assistance. Dutch troops in Kandy and naval support in the East coast enabled the Dutch to consolidate control over the entire coastline. From 1675 however, Rajasinghe recommenced attacks on the Dutch, demonstrating shrewdness in his choice of targets and eliminating much of the Dutch conquered areas. By 1677, however, the Dutch were following a policy of inactivity,\u00a0 but continued to be vigilant. And Rajasinghe in his last years of reign, sought a more reconciliatory relationship with them.\n\nRegarded as an exception among Kandyan rulers for his commitment to a policy of resistance toward the Westerners, Rajasinghe however appeared to have changed his attitude even towards those captured. The allowance as a result gave Knox to enjoy a degree of freedom unusual for a prisoner,\u00a0 and enabled him to observe Sri Lankan society with remarkable detail and sympathy.\n\nDuring the nineteen years as what was termed as a \u201cfree Prisoner,\u201d Knox cultivated crops and knitted caps spending his life as a petty trader while exploring possible escape routes."
