    "chunk_size_bytes": 1232,
    "chunk_text": "Regarded as an exception among Kandyan rulers for his commitment to a policy of resistance toward the Westerners, Rajasinghe however appeared to have changed his attitude even towards those captured. The allowance as a result gave Knox to enjoy a degree of freedom unusual for a prisoner,\u00a0 and enabled him to observe Sri Lankan society with remarkable detail and sympathy.\n\nDuring the nineteen years as what was termed as a \u201cfree Prisoner,\u201d Knox cultivated crops and knitted caps spending his life as a petty trader while exploring possible escape routes.\n\nKnox\u2019s years in captivity evidently had not diminished his appetite for travel. Following his escape, he had undertaken at least five more voyages to the East. He died in 1720 and lived long enough to bask in the well deserved popularity of his book. His prediction \u201cit being ye the only thing that will keep my name in memory in ye world,\u201d proved correct as, nearly three and a half centuries later, Robert Knox remains remembered not simply as a prisoner who escaped from the Kandyan Kingdom but as one of the most perceptive chroniclers of seventeenth century Sri Lanka whose observations continue to illuminate the island\u2019s history, the society and parlance."
