    "md5": "22ef5f50119258d29d3317491eb3e45a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Climate change, food security and need for  communication\n\nSri Lanka is uniquely positioned to contribute to this global effort. Despite its relatively small land area, Sri Lanka possesses remarkable climatic and ecological diversity. The country contains more than 50 microclimatic zones\n\nClimate change is no longer a distant environmental concern. It has become one of the greatest challenges facing humanity, affecting food production, water resources, public health, biodiversity and economic development. While scientists have warned about these risks for decades, recent evidence shows that the impacts are accelerating faster than many expected. For countries such as Sri Lanka, where agriculture remains central to livelihoods and food security, the challenge is particularly significant.\n\nThe Earth today is experiencing unprecedented environmental changes driven largely by human activities. Since the Industrial Revolution, atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO\u2082) concentrations have increased dramatically. For thousands of years, atmospheric CO\u2082 remained relatively stable at around 280\u2013350 parts per million (ppm). Today, however, it exceeds 420 ppm and continues to rise. Alongside this increase, global temperatures have risen steadily, with recent years ranking among the hottest ever recorded.\n\nThe consequences are becoming increasingly visible. Heat waves are becoming more frequent, rainfall patterns are becoming erratic, droughts are intensifying, and extreme weather events are occurring more often. These changes affect not only natural ecosystems but also the agricultural systems that provide food for billions of people.\n\nAt the same time, the world\u2019s population continues to grow. Global population has increased from about 3 billion in 1960 to more than 8 billion today and is expected to reach nearly 10 billion by 2050. This means food production must increase substantially over the coming decades despite increasingly challenging climatic conditions."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-19-dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531",
    "date_str": "2026-09-19",
    "description": "Climate change, food security and need for  communication",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Climate-change-food-security-and-need-for-communication/172-350674",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789798509.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-19-dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a41d975197426618602d1a54970b1618",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1828,
    "chunk_text": "At the same time, the world\u2019s population continues to grow. Global population has increased from about 3 billion in 1960 to more than 8 billion today and is expected to reach nearly 10 billion by 2050. This means food production must increase substantially over the coming decades despite increasingly challenging climatic conditions.\n\nHowever, the challenges facing agriculture today are far more complex. Climate change threatens to undermine many of the gains achieved during the first Green Revolution.\n\nOne of the most intriguing aspects of climate change is the dual role of carbon dioxide. CO\u2082 is both a problem and an opportunity. As the primary greenhouse gas responsible for global warming, it contributes to rising temperatures. Yet CO\u2082 is also the raw material for photosynthesis, the process by which plants convert sunlight into biomass.\n\nResearch conducted over several decades has shown that elevated CO\u2082 levels can increase photosynthesis and improve crop productivity, particularly in C\u2083 crops such as rice, wheat, and soybean. Studies conducted using Free-Air CO\u2082 Enrichment (FACE) systems have demonstrated that higher CO\u2082 concentrations can increase crop yields by improving photosynthetic efficiency and reducing water loss through stomata.\n\nThis means that crops may use water more efficiently under elevated CO\u2082 conditions. In regions where water scarcity is increasing, this benefit could partially offset some climate-related stresses.\n\nWhile elevated CO\u2082 may increase yields, rising temperatures can negate many of these gains. Research has consistently shown that high temperatures reduce grain filling, accelerate crop development, and decrease final yields. Rice, one of the world\u2019s most important staple foods and the primary food crop of Sri Lanka, is particularly vulnerable."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-19-dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531",
    "date_str": "2026-09-19",
    "description": "Climate change, food security and need for  communication",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Climate-change-food-security-and-need-for-communication/172-350674",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789798509.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-19-dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "695a9efd6cec2914735845cbcde552ec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "While elevated CO\u2082 may increase yields, rising temperatures can negate many of these gains. Research has consistently shown that high temperatures reduce grain filling, accelerate crop development, and decrease final yields. Rice, one of the world\u2019s most important staple foods and the primary food crop of Sri Lanka, is particularly vulnerable.\n\nPerhaps even more concerning is the impact of climate change on food quality.\n\nScientists have discovered that elevated CO\u2082 reduces the concentration of important nutrients in staple crops. Research on rice has revealed declines in protein, zinc, iron, and several vitamins when crops are grown under future CO\u2082 conditions.\n\nThis phenomenon has major implications for global nutrition. More than two billion people worldwide already suffer from micronutrient deficiencies, often referred to as \u201chidden hunger.\u201d Zinc deficiency affects immune function and child development, while iron deficiency remains the leading cause of anaemia worldwide.\n\nAs atmospheric CO\u2082 levels continue to rise, the nutritional quality of staple foods may decline further, increasing health risks for vulnerable populations, particularly in developing countries that depend heavily on rice and wheat.\n\nAddressing these challenges requires a new agricultural transformation\u2014a Second Green Revolution.\n\nUnlike the first Green Revolution, which focused primarily on increasing yield through improved varieties and fertilizer use, the Second Green Revolution must integrate climate resilience, digital technologies, biotechnology, precision agriculture, and sustainability.\n\nDeveloping climate-resilient crop varieties is one of the highest priorities. Future crops must tolerate drought, heat, salinity, flooding and emerging pest pressures. Advances in genomics, biotechnology, and molecular breeding provide unprecedented opportunities to accelerate crop improvement."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
