    "chunk_size_bytes": 1414,
    "chunk_text": "The operator does not lend its name lightly, he revealed. Properties that fall short of its benchmark would trade under their existing names with a \u201cmanaged by\u201d tag, and the four-star upgrade is what cleared the Trincomalee resort to carry the brand outright. \u201cIf we think it is good for our standard, then we are okay to give our name,\u201d Cader remarked.\n\nThe partners tie the timing to the east coast\u2019s momentum, citing rising tourist arrivals, expanding infrastructure and growing international interest in the region.\n\nFairway Trinco joins a leisure portfolio anchored by Fairway Colombo, a 181-room city hotel in Colombo Fort near the World Trade Centre and the Dutch Hospital, which opened in September 2016. It is billed as the first hotel in Sri Lanka to integrate robotic technology. The hotel marked 10 years of operations on 15 September and averages 85 percent occupancy, Cader noted.\n\nThe group also operates The Planter\u2019s Legacy, three private three-bedroom cottages in Nuwara Eliya around 50 metres from Lake Gregory. Its wider leisure interests include Fairway Travels, a tailor-made tour operator; Go Outdoors, which rents motor homes, caravans and trailers; and Kamu at Fort, a 10-stall food court in Colombo 01.\n\nThe leisure sector sits under Fairway Holdings, chaired by Virath de Alwis, who took the helm in 2025 following the passing of his father and group founder Hemaka de Alwis."
