    "md5": "aa31855f9797f3f098ae51a8784e2d24",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Prime lending rate edges up despite cuts by most banks\n\nThe AWPR rose 3 basis points to 10.76%, although nine of the 13 domestic banks tracked by the CB lowered their prime rates during the week\n\nThe weekly Average Weighted Prime Lending Rate (AWPR) edged up 3 basis points to 10.76 percent in the week ending 18 September, even though most of the country\u2019s larger commercial banks lowered their prime rates during the week, as per Central Bank data.\n\nThe increase partly reversed a 19 basis point decline a week earlier, when the rate fell to 10.73 percent from 10.92 percent, but left the AWPR 269 basis points above the 8.07 percent recorded a year ago. The Overnight Policy Rate, meanwhile, remains at 8.75 percent, 100 basis points higher than a year earlier.\n\nAmong domestic banks, Sampath Bank recorded the steepest reduction, lowering its prime rate by 79 basis points to 11.76 percent, followed by Seylan Bank and NDB Bank, which cut their rates by 66 and 59 basis points to 13.23 percent and 10.83 percent respectively. People\u2019s Bank, DFCC Bank and Commercial Bank also reduced their rates to 11.79 percent, 10.78 percent and 10.93 percent, while Bank of Ceylon, Hatton National Bank and Amana Bank recorded marginal cuts, leaving Bank of Ceylon and Seylan Bank with the highest prime rates in the market at 13.23 percent. In contrast, Pan Asia Banking Corporation raised its rate by 44 basis points to 11.74 percent, while Union Bank and Nations Trust Bank recorded smaller increases. Cargills Bank, which kept its rate unchanged at 10.50 percent, offered the lowest prime rate among domestic banks. Since the AWPR is weighted by loan volumes, the overall increase indicates that a larger share of prime lending during the week was extended at higher rates.\n\nAmong foreign banks, HSBC lowered its prime rate by 76 basis points to 11.63 percent and Standard Chartered by 67 basis points to 10.46 percent, while Habib Bank recorded the highest rate in the foreign bank segment at 13.06 percent."
