    "chunk_size_bytes": 1440,
    "chunk_text": "He explained that the shift towards diversification was occurring as investors questioned the risks of concentrating assets within a single jurisdiction in an increasingly divided geopolitical landscape.\n\nHe noted that gold had experienced periods of pronounced volatility, including significant declines during the first half of 2026, illustrating the potential mark-to-market impact for institutional investors.\n\nFor treasury managers whose portfolio performance feeds directly into institutional earnings, such fluctuations could make substantial gold exposure difficult to accommodate, he added.\n\nNardelli revealed that AIIB itself does not hold gold in its portfolio, although the question of whether it should do so surfaces periodically in discussions with management. He recalled a 2024 meeting with a Middle Eastern central bank that held 50 percent of its assets in gold, a level of exposure that he said had surprised him at the time. While the subsequent rally would have benefited such a position, Nardelli said recent volatility illustrated the trade-off confronting portfolio managers seeking both diversification and liquidity.\n\nHe asserted that growing geopolitical fragmentation would continue to strengthen the case for diversification across both asset classes and jurisdictions, although investors would also have to contend with the costs and operational complexities that come with a more diversified portfolio. (SAA)"
