    "chunk_text": "The flagship ODEL Mall project at Ward Place remains the group\u2019s largest exposure, with work-in-progress of Rs. 18.5 billion as at 31 March 2026. The auditors flagged its carrying value as a key audit matter, noting it represents 10 percent of group assets, and the project recorded a Rs. 370 million impairment during the year. Management has scrapped the original apartment component in favour of office space and prioritised Phase I, retaining around 100 perches of land for future development. The company intends to raise Rs. 6.1 billion through office space pre-sales and external equity to complete construction. Meanwhile, a bank consortium led by Hatton National Bank is evaluating options to restructure the existing syndicated loan.\n\nLeisure and property revenue rose 5 percent to Rs. 3.1 billion, while sector EBITDA improved to Rs. 303 million from Rs. 74 million.\n\nThe automobile sector delivered one of the sharpest turnarounds following the relaxation of vehicle import restrictions. Sector revenue climbed to Rs. 3.5 billion from Rs. 409 million, and the business posted a profit after tax of Rs. 231 million against a loss of Rs. 111 million a year earlier. The recovery came from the return of Ford vehicles and Suzuki motorcycles, along with the group\u2019s entry into the passenger and commercial three-wheeler market through ATUL Auto."
