    "md5": "8dc81817d4bd52dd2c131cd9d785a8a5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1714,
    "chunk_text": "Dangerous,  shifting world\n\nIn other words, the rapid rise of financial institutions including the encroachment of finance into every sector has produced mounting crises.\n\nThere is an even more insidious danger with the possibility of a financial meltdown with the bursting of the AI bubble\n\nAmidst this tumultuous world, Sri Lanka needs to rapidly change its economic policies. We need to put up barriers to the devastating flows of capital rather than go along with the IMF and its recommendations for further financial opening\n\nThe Sri Lankan economy is not an island. We like to think that all will be fine as long as we stabilise our economy and ensure fiscal discipline. However, in reality, economic crises in Sri Lanka have more often been triggered by external shocks rather than internal problems. And now we are staring at economic and political developments that signal a dangerous world.\n\nThere are different external shocks that have been in the making, and it is indeed difficult to predict as to which particular source or combination of factors can rock the global order. The war in Iran has again intensified, global oil prices are hovering around US$ 100 per barrel, shipping around the Middle East is in trouble and the region itself could spiral into escalating wars.\n\nThe racist political right is on the rise in Europe, including in France and Germany that hold Europe together. Global trade is precarious with Trump\u2019s arbitrary tariffs over the last year and a half. Indeed, with the trade system in disarray, the possibility of an export-led economic strategy has also become questionable, because even if there is increase in comparative productivity, tariffs will nullify the advances."
  },
