    "chunk_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-9e2a72da-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "10d657154ee6134732d0865561d20c3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1564,
    "chunk_text": "The racist political right is on the rise in Europe, including in France and Germany that hold Europe together. Global trade is precarious with Trump\u2019s arbitrary tariffs over the last year and a half. Indeed, with the trade system in disarray, the possibility of an export-led economic strategy has also become questionable, because even if there is increase in comparative productivity, tariffs will nullify the advances.\n\nThere is an even more insidious danger with the possibility of a financial meltdown with the bursting of the AI bubble. It is the crisis tendencies generated by the global financial system, of which we know little about and hard to analyse, that we need to be particularly concerned.\n\nThe last five decades of neoliberal policies \u2013 what are commonly known in Sri Lanka as open economy reforms \u2013 have led to both increased financialisation of the economies and repeated financial crises. In other words, the rapid rise of financial institutions including the encroachment of finance into every sector has produced mounting crises caused by increased sovereign debt and capital flight. Notable are the Latin American and African debt crisis of the 1980s, the East Asian crisis of 1997, the North Atlantic financial crisis of 2008, the Greek sovereign debt crisis of the 2010s, and the Global South debt crisis of the early 2020s; the latter as we know crippled our country. These are symptomatic of a global financial system that creates tremendous wealth for global financiers but leads to dispossession for the rest of the population."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-9e2a72da",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-9e2a72da",
