    "md5": "1b954c3628e1a8dc1c3f98f774523868",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1643,
    "chunk_text": "Twenty-five years later, I sense similar sentiments and developments with the AI boom. The financial sector has been leveraged to the hilt for speculative investments in firms and data centres that for the most part are still loss making and can hardly show any real revenues. With every effort to hype up AI running out of steam, the time is ripe for a crash. But very few people seem to remember the past or learn from it. Indeed, finance moves quickly these days, but it also seems to make us forget quickly.\n\nOne may believe that this may just be another crisis for global capital that in a year or two will recover like all the other crises during the neoliberal era. However, I argue that this time it is different because neoliberalism itself is in crisis. Ideologically and materially, free markets are quickly crumbling as a polarised world not just in terms of political outlooks but also in terms of trade wars, if not physical wars, which are making any coordinated response by global capital to save itself unworkable.\n\nIn other words, I claim that the consequences of another bond market crash or the AI bubble bursting will be far worse than the previous financial crises. The confluence of crisis tendencies may create a downward cycle of unravelling that even the Central Banks and Finance Ministries of the most powerful countries will be unable to address; all the more so, as these states are at loggerheads with each other. This global political situation is more akin to the situation in the 1930s, when the resolution to the crisis came only after a prolonged depression, the rise of fascism and a devastating world war."
  },
