    "chunk_text": "\u201cElections are fundamental to democratic societies. Supporting citizens to participate in elections while living abroad is an issue many election management bodies continue to consider and navigate. Overseas voting raises a range of legal, policy, administrative, technological and operational considerations. Different countries have approached these questions in ways that reflect their own circumstances, institutions and priorities,\u201d she added.\n\nThe NPP government has appointed a Parliamentary Select Committee to initiate the process of establishing an OCV mechanism for Sri Lankans living overseas, said Minister of Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government Chandana Abeyratne.\n\nHe said that the government has demonstrated its commitment to democracy by conducting free and fair elections while maintaining public confidence in the country\u2019s democratic process. \u201cNPP\u2019s electoral framework aims to build a more democratic, transparent and inclusive system of representation in Sri Lanka. Over 1.5 million citizens live and work overseas. Their relationship with Sri Lanka extends beyond geographical distances. As we consider expanding facilities for our citizens residing overseas to participate in elections and exercise their democratic rights, we must ensure that such measures safeguard electoral integrity, security, transparency and public confidence in the electoral process,\u201d he added.\n\nAs we consider expanding facilities for our citizens residing overseas to participate in elections and exercise their democratic rights, we must ensure that such measures safeguard electoral integrity, security, transparency and public confidence in the electoral process\u201d-\n\nChandana Abeyratne, Minister of Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government\n\nOCV models implemented in several regional countries are as follows ;"
