    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "As we consider expanding facilities for our citizens residing overseas to participate in elections and exercise their democratic rights, we must ensure that such measures safeguard electoral integrity, security, transparency and public confidence in the electoral process\u201d-\n\nChandana Abeyratne, Minister of Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government\n\nOCV models implemented in several regional countries are as follows ;\n\nAustralia - In 2025, the Australian Elections Commission (AEC) delivered services to a relatively small overseas electorate of roughly 17,000 voters across 83 countries with 101 polling places. The AEC relies on in-person voting at diplomatic missions and postal voting supported by a hand-to-hand courier service.\n\nMaldives - Maldivian OCV is automatically open to all citizens over 18 based on permanent residency. However, two-thirds of the population does not reside at their permanent address, living instead in the capital (Mal\u00e9), industrial zones, tourist resorts, or overseas. To resolve this, the election management body introduced a dynamic registration layer, allowing voters to choose their polling destination. To manage costs, the Maldives established strict minimum thresholds: for example, a polling station was set up in domestic resorts/industrial zones if a minimum of 30 voters registered.\n\nPhilippines - Guided by the 1987 Constitution, the Philippines enacted Republic Act 9189 (Overseas Absentee Voting Act of 2003), which was later amended by Republic Act 10590 in 2013 which granted the Commission of Elections broad powers to explore electronic and internet-based OCV. Marking a historic milestone, the 2025 midterm pilot enrolled 234,475 pre-voting enrollees (19.2% of registered overseas voters) and implemented internet voting across 77 of 93 foreign posts, covering more than 1.2 million registered voters."
