    "description": "Govt considers allowing private firms to set fuel prices",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Govt-considers-allowing-private-firms-to-set-fuel-prices/108-350778",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789969258.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-6b10717f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3ab9a9d0fc795f542cf38840396d6308",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "Govt considers allowing private firms to set fuel prices\n\nColombo, Sep. 21 (Daily Mirror) - The government is examining legal options to allow private fuel distributors to set their own prices within a specified upper and lower range, Energy Minister Anura Karunathilaka said.\n\nHe said the move is being considered as several private fuel distribution companies have raised concerns over heavy losses under the existing pricing structure, leading to restrictions on diesel supplies.\n\nThree private fuel companies in Sri Lanka\u2014Lanka IOC (LIOC), Sinopec, and RM Parks\u2014have restricted diesel distribution by about 50% due to severe financial losses caused by surging global oil costs and the removal of the government diesel subsidy. The operators are lobbying the Ministry of Power and Energy for an immediate price revision to prevent potential station closures.\n\nThe Minister said the government will also conduct its monthly fuel price review at the end of September, based on fuel import costs.\n\nThe Energy Minister said the Middle East conflict has pushed global fuel prices higher after a period of lower prices that allowed Sri Lanka to pass some savings on to consumers.\n\nHowever, he said that fuel prices are already significantly high and said discussions are underway on whether consumers can bear any further price increase.\n\n\u201cWe review fuel prices based on import costs and expect to conduct the next review at the end of September. However, fuel prices are already significantly high, and the government is discussing whether consumers can bear any further increases.\n\nSeveral private fuel distributors have also said they cannot absorb the losses under the current pricing structure, resulting in restricted diesel supplies. We are therefore examining legal options to allow these companies to set prices within a specified upper and lower range,\u201d the Minister said."
  },
