    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi group launched missile and drone strikes at the Saudi capital of Riyadh on Sept. 19, after having made sudden military gains in Yemen's reignited civil war earlier this month. Video published by Reuters showed a massive plume of black smoke billowing from the area of Riyadh's main international airport following booms overnight.\n\nWith Iran already having blockaded most shipping in the Strait of Hormuz, the oil-rich Gulf's energy exports are now almost entirely cut off from global markets, adding to pressure on oil prices.\n\nDespite the economic damage, there is little sign of reviving the interim ceasefire struck in June that fell apart in July amid disputes over its terms.\n\nThe oil price crunch has sent diesel prices to record highs, causing pain for drivers at the pump.\n\nThe national average for diesel hit over $6.50 per gallon, the latest record high according to AAA. The price of a gallon of regular gas topped $4.47, more than $1.29 higher than a year ago.\n\nThe war has cost the U.S. military at least $43.6 billion as of Sept. 3, according to an update shared with congressional national security committees by U.S. Central Command.\n\nThe cost update is almost certainly an undercount, according to previous USA TODAY reporting. It does not include costs to repair buildings at U.S. bases across the Middle East that were damaged or destroyed by Iranian strikes during the war, including the U.S. Navy\u2019s logistics hub in Bahrain.\n\nThe Pentagon estimated it has spent $28.1 billion to replace expended munitions like bombs and air defense interceptors. The prolonged conflict in the Middle East has caused a shortage of critical munitions that could leave the United States vulnerable in other parts of the world, such as the Indo-Pacific.\n\nAt least 18 Americans have died, and more than 800 have been wounded since the start of the conflict."
  },
