    "md5": "2c0ce8c206c08b6eba56a1fa0024ac82",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1799,
    "chunk_text": "President pledges tax relief in 2027 Budget\n\nColombo, Sep. 21 (Daily Mirror) - Acknowledging the tax burden faced by the public, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake yesterday said the Government was expecting to remove certain taxes through the 2027 Budget to provide relief to people affected by rising costs.\n\nAddressing a public rally in Akuressa, the President said the Government had focused on strengthening the economy, livelihoods, food security and opportunities for economic advancement, while stressing the need to translate these efforts into tangible benefits for the people.\n\nHe said the Government had planned programmes to channel funds collected by the Treasury to communities at the grassroots level. According to the President, Rs. 2 billion has been allocated for capital expenditure in next year's Budget.\n\nThe Government would also take measures to support vulnerable sections of society, he said, emphasizing the need to ensure that relief reaches those who need it most.\n\n\u201cEven in developed countries, certain groups of people live on relief. Funds in the Treasury must be channeled to the village level as development and as relief as well,\u201d he said.\n\nReferring to rising international fuel prices, President Dissanayake said fuel prices in the global market had increased and that, based on the new prices, domestic fuel prices would have to be revised from October 1.\n\nHowever, he said the Government had decided to provide concessions to minimixe the impact on consumers.\n\n\u201cWe allocated Rs. 600 billion to provide fuel concessions in April, May, June and July. The Government must give a concession if fuel prices are increased unbearably,\u201d he said.\n\nThe President said the Government was also giving priority to the development of education, health and transport."
  },
