    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c4efd1086a03ed847a9cb404921bf788",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "Pereira maintained he was only a transit passenger, that the gold was destined for India and that he had never \u201carrived\u201d in Sri Lanka in the legal sense. He later claimed ownership after initially telling Customs officers the gold belonged to a third party in Dubai and that he was merely a courier for a fee.\n\nThe Attorney General appealed. On the question of ownership, the Supreme Court held that Pereira had failed to establish title. His original statement to Customs that the gold had been given to him by a man named Mohammed for delivery in Madras against a fee was never satisfactorily explained. The Court rejected the later assertion of ownership as an afterthought required only to meet the statutory requirement that a claim under the Customs Ordinance must be made by the owner. Section 110 of the Evidence Ordinance, which creates a presumption of ownership from possession, was held inapplicable because possession of the gold without a permit was unlawful.\n\nOn the second issue, the Court ruled that the bringing of gold into Sri Lanka constitutes importation under Section 21 of the then Exchange Control Act, even when the passenger remains in transit and does not clear immigration. Section 21(2) expressly deems the bringing of gold into a port or airport, even if it is intended to leave Sri Lanka without being removed from the vessel or aircraft to be importation. The Court noted that gold was a restricted item requiring prior Central Bank permission. Once imported contrary to that restriction, the gold became liable to forfeiture under Section 43 of the Customs Ordinance.\n\nThe case, which began with a plaint filed in the District Court of Colombo in September 1991, has spanned more than three decades, successive appeals and the substitution of a new plaintiff after the original claimant\u2019s death."
  },
