    "chunk_size_bytes": 1585,
    "chunk_text": "\u2019\u2019It is your bilateral issue, we, unlike Western countries, don\u2019t put pressure\u2019\u2019 - Russian Ambassador\n\nColombo, Sep. 21 (Daily Mirror) - In response to a query about Sri Lanka seeking defence cooperation with the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation (NATO), Russian Ambassador Levan Dzhagaryan said that it was Sri Lanka's bilateral issue, and Russia, unlike Western countries, does not put pressure.\n\nAsked whether Russia is concerned about Sri Lanka's engagement with NATO, the Ambassador told Daily Mirror, \" No, it\u2019s you bilateral issue. Unlike Western countries, we don\u2019t put pressure.\"\n\nDefence Ministry Secretary Air Vice Marshal (Retd) Sampath Thuyacontha held bilateral talks with NATO Military Committee Chairperson Admiral Giuseppe Cavo Dragone on the sidelines of the Chiefs of Defence Conference in Victoria, Canada recently.\n\nDiscussions focused on military modernization, cyber readiness, advanced technology, and disaster response\n\nThey also focused on upgrading hardware, acquiring new technologies, enhancing surveillance, and expanding personnel training and information sharing.\n\nThuyacontha also highlighted humanitarian assistance and disaster relief cooperation, including the use of drones in emergency operations, and invited NATO to strengthen long-term engagement with Sri Lanka.\n\nNATO is a political and military alliance of countries from Europe and North America. Its members are committed to protecting each other from any threat.\n\nNATO-Russia relations are currently in a state of deep crisis, following the full-scale war involving Ukraine."
  },
