    "md5": "d8913c61099d4b5b178f943fae32042b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1998,
    "chunk_text": "Iran must stop arming Houthis in Yemen, G7 says ahead of UN General Assembly\n\nBBC - Iran must \"end its arming of and support\" for the Houthis in Yemen, the foreign ministers of G7 nations have said in a joint statement.\n\nThe G7 also condemned \"in the strongest terms\" Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia, adding that the recent escalation in fighting against Saudi-backed forces in Yemen represented a threat to regional and global energy security.\n\nThousands of refugees have fled Yemen in recent days after the Iran-backed group seized territory along the country's Red Sea coastline, including near the Bab al-Mandab Strait, a critical shipping route for global trade.\n\nThe statement comes ahead of the annual UN General Assembly in New York, where senior Iranian officials are due to speak.\n\n\"We call on the Houthis to immediately cease all military actions, all threats and attacks against civilian shipping, and to return to the political process in good faith,\" said the foreign ministers of the UK, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States.\n\nTheir statement was issued following a meeting on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly.\n\nMeanwhile, at least six people were killed in Saudi strikes on the strategic Yemeni city of Mokha, the AFP news agency reported, citing Houthi-affiliated media.\n\nTwo children and a woman were among the dead, while eight others were injured in the attack on the city, which the Houthis seized from pro-government forces earlier this month.\n\nSaudi Arabia, which backs the internationally recognised government of Yemen, has not yet commented on the reported strikes.\n\nThe UN estimates that in all, more than 120,000 Yemenis have been displaced by the sudden surge in fighting along Yemen's Red Sea coast.\n\nEarlier this month, the Houthis, who had already seized most of the populated parts of Yemen, took control of Bab al-Mandab, a narrow, maritime chokepoint where the southern Red Sea flows into the Gulf of Aden and the wider Indian Ocean."
  },
