    "description": "Govt. commits to boat manufacturing zone; EDB to pick site",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Govt-commits-to-boat-manufacturing-zone-EDB-to-pick-site/273-350856",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790054969.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-22-dailymirrorlk-c46d0844-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "95a3c2e822a61ec3053b92647df0ff4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. commits to boat manufacturing zone; EDB to pick site\n\nEDB targets over US $ 500mn in marine exports, over 9 times 2025 boat export earnings\n\nOnly 10 to 12 of 60 to 70 boatbuilders are established exporters\n\nThe government has committed to establishing a dedicated boat manufacturing zone, with the Export Development Board (EDB) in the process of selecting a site from the locations proposed by the industry, the speakers said at the fourth Annual General Meeting of the Chamber of Marine Industries of Sri Lanka (CMISL), held at Amari Colombo last week.\n\nOutgoing CMISL Chairman Indhra Kaushal Rajapaksa revealed that the chamber had put forward the Oruwella Harbour and Kapparathota Harbour Marina as potential locations, following a recent meeting with the Industry and Fisheries Ministers, at which the government agreed to set up the zone.\n\nHe explained that the industry\u2019s main difficulty lies in shipping out the completed vessels rather than in building them.\n\n\u201cWe find it very difficult not to make boats but to export the finished boat,\u201d Rajapaksa told the gathering, adding that the Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Ministry had agreed to set up a one-stop shop within the ministry to help the boat exporters resolve their problems.\n\nNewly appointed CMISL Chairman Ricky Barnett observed that a dedicated zone would lower the production costs for the local builders and could attract foreign direct investment by improving the feasibility of projects in the sector.\n\nThe zone marks a second attempt at such a facility, as Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Ministry Secretary J.M. Thilaka Jayasundara acknowledged that an earlier effort with the Sri Lanka Ports Authority to set aside a separate industrial zone for maritime, boatbuilding and shipbuilding activities had collapsed."
  },
