    "chunk_id": "2026-09-22-dailymirrorlk-e5a69493-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ef336864c2d1325ed5a78457899a05fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1621,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo transshipment climbs as adverse weather threatens to worsen port congestion\n\nTransshipment rose 7.8% YoY to 638,843 TEUs in August, accounting for 82.3% of total throughput\n\nTransshipment for first 8 months grew 10.9% to 4.83mn TEUs, driving nearly 85% of volume growth\n\nCongestion has eased from its mid-September peak but could worsen over next 2 days due to adverse weather\n\nThe transshipment volumes at the Port of Colombo rose 7.8 percent year-on-year (YoY) to 638,843 TEUs in August, lifting their share of total container throughput to 82.3 percent, from 79.9 percent a year earlier, while the overall container handling grew at a slower 4.7 percent to 776,300 TEUs, according to Sri Lanka Ports Authority (SLPA) data.\n\nTransshipment added 46,430 TEUs during the month, more than the port\u2019s entire net gain of 35,176 TEUs, as the domestic cargo moved in the opposite direction. The laden imports fell 16.3 percent to 45,769 TEUs and the laden exports declined 4.4 percent to 26,896 TEUs. This contrasts with the eight-month period, when the laden imports were up 4.9 percent at 429,160 TEUs and the laden exports up 2.0 percent at 208,004 TEUs, suggesting the softening in local trade is recent and has been masked by the surge in the relay cargo.\n\nFor the first eight months, the port handled 5,994,847 TEUs, up 10.3 percent, with transshipment of 4,827,968 TEUs accounting for 474,983 TEUs or nearly 85 percent, of the 560,009 TEU increase. At this pace, Colombo is set to comfortably surpass its 2025 record of 8.29 million TEUs and end the year at nearly nine million TEUs, around 8.5 percent higher."
  },
