    "md5": "2684a21a7e883234aeb1b24d60e32659",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1544,
    "chunk_text": "For the first eight months, the port handled 5,994,847 TEUs, up 10.3 percent, with transshipment of 4,827,968 TEUs accounting for 474,983 TEUs or nearly 85 percent, of the 560,009 TEU increase. At this pace, Colombo is set to comfortably surpass its 2025 record of 8.29 million TEUs and end the year at nearly nine million TEUs, around 8.5 percent higher.\n\nTotal ship arrivals edged up 1.4 percent to 353 in August, even as the container vessel calls fell 5.9 percent to 286, while the bunkering calls nearly doubled to 29, from 15.\n\nAt the CICT, the container vessel calls dropped 19.8 percent to 77, while the volumes held steady, raising the average exchange per call by 24 percent to around 3,404 TEUs.\n\nTotal cargo handled declined 4.2 percent to 9.25 million tonnes, with the containerised cargo down 6.7 percent to 8.44 million tonnes, although the tonnage for the SAGT and CICT is estimated. Over eight months, the ship arrivals rose 2.8 percent to 2,780 and the cargo handled grew 2.8 percent to 80.04 million tonnes.\n\nMeanwhile, an industry source told Mirror Business that the congestion has eased but not fully cleared, with the vessel turnaround time at the CICT down to four to five days from around seven days earlier.\n\nThe yard operations at the port are still running at 125 percent of capacity, the source noted, attributing the pressure primarily to the Gulf cargo diverted from Jebel Ali, amid the Middle East tensions, as reported earlier by Mirror Business, while a shortage of tugs continues to slow the vessel movements."
