    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Is another Ditwah-like situation around the corner?\n\nColombo, Sep. 22 (Daily Mirror) - With memories of Cyclone Ditwah\u2019s devastating floods and landslides still fresh, Sri Lanka could be heading into a critical October-November period, with meteorologists warning that El Ni\u00f1o conditions would intensify the country\u2019s usual rainfall, strong winds and landslide risks.\n\nSpeaking to Daily Mirror, Deputy Director of Forecasting and Early Warning at the Meteorological Department, J.W. Karunaratna, said the prevailing rainy conditions could intensify further with the developing El Ni\u00f1o conditions.\n\nA low-pressure area located over the Andaman Sea region of the Bay of Bengal is not expected to directly affect the island, however, the wind flow towards the system is expected to keep rainy conditions over the southwestern parts of the country.\n\nHe said even though there could be a slight reduction in rainfall after September 23, heavier rains above 100mm could return after September 27, making the coming period particularly important.\n\n\u201cOctober and November are critical months in terms of strong winds and landslides. We normally receive rain during this period, but with the El Ni\u00f1o situation, the conditions could be worse,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cAlthough it may not reach the magnitude of Ditwah, the impact could still be colossal,\u201d Karunaratna said.\n\nMeanwhile, the Irrigation Department has warned of rising river levels and possible minor flooding in several areas following significant rainfall across the wet zone.\n\nIrrigation Director L.S. Sooriyabandara said rainfall had eased slightly compared to previous days, but showers were continuing.\n\nThe upper reaches of the Kelani River received between 100 mm and 125 mm of rain by this morning, while many other river basins recorded between 50 mm and 80 mm during the past 24 hours, he said."
