    "md5": "7d991f8eb0952e1a95c1f31b394ef3c3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1072,
    "chunk_text": "Irrigation Director L.S. Sooriyabandara said rainfall had eased slightly compared to previous days, but showers were continuing.\n\nThe upper reaches of the Kelani River received between 100 mm and 125 mm of rain by this morning, while many other river basins recorded between 50 mm and 80 mm during the past 24 hours, he said.\n\nThe Kuda Ganga, a tributary of the Kalu Ganga, has also risen at Millakanda and is nearing minor flood levels.\n\nWater levels in the Kelani River at Hanwella, Lenkoss and Avissawella remain high, while levels in the Mahaweli River at Nawalapitiya and Peradeniya and the Attanagalu Oya have declined but remain close to risk thresholds.\n\nThe latest developments come as Sri Lanka continues to recover from the devastating impact of Cyclone Ditwah, which triggered widespread flooding and landslides across the country last year.\n\nDitwah brought exceptionally heavy rainfall, causing rivers to overflow and triggering thousands of landslides, while affecting millions of people and causing extensive damage to homes, infrastructure and livelihoods."
