    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d164d1221d38a34aff78c78d949f05a3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2015,
    "chunk_text": "It also backed wealth taxation as part of a more equitable revenue framework, arguing that those with a greater ability to pay should contribute a larger share.\n\nThe recommendations come as Sri Lanka faces a tighter fiscal framework in 2027 following temporary easing this year.\n\nThe IMF has similarly emphasised broadening the tax base and improving compliance. Following a visit in June, IMF staff said Sri Lanka should continue efforts to improve tax compliance, broaden the tax base and strengthen public financial management.\n\nThe Government has committed under the programme to approve revenue measures supporting fiscal consolidation in 2027 and to develop a Medium-Term Revenue Strategy informed by a tax diagnostic. The IMF programme documentation says the strategy is intended to make taxation more efficient and equitable through broader tax bases, rationalised tax expenditures and improved administration and compliance.\n\nIPS\u2019 proposals, however, underline the need to balance that revenue requirement against the risk of further squeezing households and businesses through higher tax rates.\n\nThe institute pointed to the transformation in Sri Lanka\u2019s revenue position since the crisis, while arguing that the composition and efficiency of taxation now require greater attention.\n\nThe fiscal adjustment has also come at a cost to productive government spending. IPS noted that public investment fell from around 5 percent to 3 percent of GDP during the adjustment period, even as high public debt and limited fiscal space continued to constrain policy choices.\n\nThis creates a difficult policy trade-off for Budget 2027: maintaining sufficient revenue to protect debt sustainability while ensuring fiscal consolidation does not continue to crowd out expenditure capable of lifting the economy\u2019s productive capacity.\n\nIPS said expenditure rationalisation should therefore move beyond simply controlling spending and focus on improving the Government\u2019s capacity to select and execute investments."
  },
