    "md5": "78039fde1714c0da3c4504d329e281ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "LMFN chief urges microfinance sector to embrace regulation as ally of growth\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s microfinance institutions must view regulation and growth as two expressions of the same commitment rather than competing forces, as the industry braces for a new era of formal oversight, Lanka Microfinance Network (LMFN) Chairman Danushka Udugama told sector leaders gathered in Bolgoda last week.\n\nHe made these remarks at the Microfinance Leaders\u2019 Forum 2026, the network\u2019s flagship annual gathering held under the theme \u201cAn Evening Gathering: Leading Through a Regulated Future\u201d on 11 September at Hotel Clio, Bolgoda.\n\nThe forum drew Chief Executive Officers, board members and senior management from microfinance institutions across the island, in what organisers described as one of the sector\u2019s most significant gatherings in recent years.\n\n\u201cAs our industry moves into a new regulatory era, evenings like this remind us that regulation and growth are not competing forces, they are two expressions of the same commitment: to protect our clients and strengthen the institutions that serve them,\u201d Udugama noted.\n\nThanking those in attendance, he added, \u201cI want to thank every CEO, board member and partner who set aside their evening to stand together as one microfinance family. Tonight, we didn\u2019t just talk about a regulated future, we started building it, together.\u201d\n\nDelivering the keynote address on \u201cThe Role of CEOs in Leading Institutional Transformation,\u201d Dr. Kishu Gomes called on industry leaders to approach the coming regulatory transition as an opportunity to strengthen governance and rebuild public trust in the sector, rather than treating it as a constraint on growth."
  },
