    "md5": "922e2a65b1aa18fec9d346506a4d5a56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1714,
    "chunk_text": "The rating action is therefore likely to be most valuable as a confidence signal. It tells investors and lenders that Sri Lanka\u2019s economic direction has improved, while placing greater pressure on the government to avoid reversing the policies that supported the recovery.\n\nFitch forecast a primary Budget surplus of 2.6 percent of gross domestic product in 2026, down from a record 5.4 percent in 2025. The primary balance excludes interest payments and is a key measure of whether government revenue can cover non-interest expenditure.\n\nMaintaining a primary surplus is critical for Sri Lanka because it allows the government to gradually reduce debt rather than borrowing further to fund its regular operations.\n\nThe overall Budget deficit is nevertheless expected to widen to 4.1 percent of GDP in 2026 from 2.3 percent in 2025, partly reflecting reconstruction expenditure following Cyclone Ditwah and targeted energy-support measures.\n\nGovernment revenue has been strengthened by tax reforms and a temporary surge in import duties following the release of pent-up demand for imported vehicles.\n\nAlthough vehicle-related revenue is expected to moderate, Fitch forecast government collections to remain just below 16 percent of GDP over the coming years. It expects primary surpluses to remain above 2 percent, supported by revenue mobilisation and expenditure restraint.\n\nThe Public Financial Management Act limits non-interest expenditure to 13 percent of GDP through 2031, providing a legal anchor for fiscal discipline. The limit has, however, been exceeded in 2026 due to cyclone reconstruction spending.\n\nFitch also warned that the risk of fiscal slippage could increase as the 2029 elections approach."
  },
