    "md5": "198dafa326c92f30f550cfcf5c011071",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s dependence on imported fuel and fertiliser remains a structural weakness, leaving its external accounts exposed to geopolitical conflict and global commodity-price shocks.\n\nFitch expects the current account to return to near balance in 2027 as the energy shock eases.\n\nThat would cover about 2.9 months of current external payments, still a relatively thin buffer for an economy with a history of foreign-exchange shortages.\n\nThe modest reserve position means another prolonged oil-price shock, a fall in tourism receipts or a weakening of remittance inflows could quickly place pressure on the rupee and external liquidity.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s most difficult test may arrive after the current period of multilateral support.\n\nExternal debt repayments are expected to rise over the next five years, particularly after 2028. Fitch also expects the highest threshold attached to the country\u2019s macro-linked bonds to be triggered, resulting in higher interest and principal payments.\n\nThe government is considering a return to global bond markets in 2027, while the present IMF Extended Fund Facility is due to end in March next year.\n\nA \u2018B-\u2019 rating makes a future market return more credible than it was under \u2018CCC+\u2019. However, borrowing at sustainable interest rates will depend on global financial conditions, investor confidence and Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to demonstrate that its debt trajectory remains manageable.\n\nEntering international markets too early or accepting excessively high yields could recreate the refinancing risks that contributed to the previous crisis.\n\nFitch said a follow-on IMF programme remains possible. Such an arrangement could offer a financing backstop and preserve reform discipline as debt repayments increase.\n\nFitch forecast economic growth to slow to 4.1 percent in 2026 from an average of 5 percent over the previous two years, before settling at slightly above 4 percent over the medium term."
