    "md5": "f218904ea7539577d82079796c17c0d3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1721,
    "chunk_text": "Is Nature Finally Striking Back at Us?\n\nAs natural disasters strike with increasing frequency, humanity faces a critical question: is nature striking back? While scientifically passive, natural systems are behaving unpredictably due to human intervention. Decades of deforestation, wetland destruction, and climate disruption have dismantled nature\u2019s defenses. Ultimately, nature is not seeking revenge, but simply presenting humanity with the inevitable bill for its arrogance.\n\n\u201cThe central message is brutally simple: nature does not need us, but we need nature.\u201d\n\n\u201cSo while the rain causes the flooding, our own actions may have helped turn that flood into a disaster.\u201d\n\n\u201cSo perhaps nature is not striking back at us after all... what we are seeing is simply nature presenting us with the bill.\u201d\n\nOf late, one cannot help feeling that natural disasters are occurring around us with increasing frequency.\n\nAlmost every time we switch on the television or look at the news and social media, there seems to be another natural disaster somewhere in the world.\n\nSri Lanka has had its own share, with the most recent being the devastating effects of Ditwah and subsequent extreme weather. Nepal has experienced destructive floods and landslides, while the Philippines, Myanmar, Afghanistan, Bangladesh, Venezuela and Colombia have all faced different natural calamities in recent times.\n\nFloods, cyclones, landslides, droughts, wildfires and extreme heat seem to be occurring with regularity. Earthquakes have added to the destruction in some countries, although they belong to a very different geological process.\n\nAgainst this background, a serious question inevitably arises: is nature striking back at us?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
