    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-bc3d8475-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bc47bffd78e2b68ae6ac2ee1c731ad44",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1463,
    "chunk_text": "Floods, cyclones, landslides, droughts, wildfires and extreme heat seem to be occurring with regularity. Earthquakes have added to the destruction in some countries, although they belong to a very different geological process.\n\nAgainst this background, a serious question inevitably arises: is nature striking back at us?\n\nThere is something chilling about this because we humans have spent much of our relatively short existence behaving as though the opposite were true. Somewhere along the way we seem to have convinced ourselves that we are all-powerful and in full control.\n\nPerhaps we can even be excused for this arrogance. After all, have we not sent men to the moon and brought them safely back? We have split the atom, changed DNA, cloned mammals and, more recently, grown human embryo-like models in laboratories using stem cells, without the normal process of fertilisation involving sperm and an egg. We can transplant hearts, communicate instantly across the world, look billions of years back into the universe. And most recently we have now built machines capable of forms of reasoning that, until very recently, we thought belonged only to us.\u00a0 \u00a0These are astonishing achievements by any measure, and perhaps it is understandable that somewhere along this extraordinary journey of human advancement, we began to believe that there was very little beyond our control. In fact, we may perhaps be excused for occasionally thinking that we are God."
  },
