    "md5": "53f204d6034e619b2c1d491e005ac87d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1329,
    "chunk_text": "Perhaps we can even be excused for this arrogance. After all, have we not sent men to the moon and brought them safely back? We have split the atom, changed DNA, cloned mammals and, more recently, grown human embryo-like models in laboratories using stem cells, without the normal process of fertilisation involving sperm and an egg. We can transplant hearts, communicate instantly across the world, look billions of years back into the universe. And most recently we have now built machines capable of forms of reasoning that, until very recently, we thought belonged only to us.\u00a0 \u00a0These are astonishing achievements by any measure, and perhaps it is understandable that somewhere along this extraordinary journey of human advancement, we began to believe that there was very little beyond our control. In fact, we may perhaps be excused for occasionally thinking that we are God.\n\nPerhaps our scientific achievements have therefore given us a rather dangerous illusion. We have become very good at manipulating parts of nature and, somewhere along the way, begun confusing that ability with mastery over nature itself.\n\nScientifically, of course, the answer has to be no. Nature does not have feelings, become angry with us or decide that humans have misbehaved and therefore send a cyclone, flood or landslide as punishment."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
