    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-bc3d8475-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "79bc9e7e0eda7617f9c82910788329f8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1517,
    "chunk_text": "Scientifically, of course, the answer has to be no. Nature does not have feelings, become angry with us or decide that humans have misbehaved and therefore send a cyclone, flood or landslide as punishment.\n\nBut that certainly does not exonerate us by any means, because we have definitely altered the conditions in which many natural events now occur. Human-induced climate change has warmed the atmosphere, oceans and land, and the evidence is now very strong that this warming is influencing many weather and climate extremes.\n\nRising sea levels worsen coastal flooding and storm surges, while prolonged heat and drought can leave landscapes much more vulnerable to wildfires. (2021, IPCC AR6 Working Group I- \u201cWeather and Climate Extreme Events in a Changing Climate\u201d)\u00a0 \u00a0However, long before climate change became the great environmental issue of our time, we were already busy dismantling many of nature\u2019s own defences.\n\nFor generations we have cut forests from mountain slopes, filled wetlands, destroyed mangroves, interfered with rivers, fragmented ecosystems and covered enormous areas of land with buildings, roads and concrete. When the rains come and mountainsides collapse, we then call it a natural disaster. Forests that once slowed runoff and helped hold soil together have disappeared from many areas, and we seem surprised when torrential rain sends water, mud and debris rushing down the mountains. The recent landslides after heavy rains in many of our upcountry areas are classic examples."
  },
