    "chunk_text": "5,741-km elephant fence gets major resource boost\n\nColombo, Sep. 23 (Daily Mirror) - The Government has launched a major programme to strengthen the resources available to wildlife officials responsible for maintaining Sri Lanka\u2019s 5,741-kilometre electric fence network, as part of a long-term strategy to address the country\u2019s human-elephant conflict.\n\nThe initiative, spearheaded by the Ministry of Environment, is based on the policy that effective management of the conflict requires frontline wildlife officials to be provided with adequate human and physical resources.\n\nThe human-elephant conflict has developed over several decades and has been linked to factors including unplanned development and habitat fragmentation. The Government said the latest programme seeks to move away from temporary measures towards a scientific, data-driven and long-term approach.\n\nThe allocation of resources has been made following assessments of the extent of electric fencing across the country, the intensity of human and elephant casualties and the terrain conditions in different areas.\n\nUnder the programme, modern motorcycles have been provided to wildlife regulators to strengthen regular supervision and maintenance of electric fences. The objective is to enable one regulator to oversee an average of around 40 kilometres of fencing.\n\nA total of 36 motorcycles have been allocated to Polonnaruwa, 22 to the Eastern region, 17 to Anuradhapura, 13 to Trincomalee, 12 to the Southern region and 12 to the North-Western region.\n\nThe vehicles are expected to enable officers to reach fence locations quickly, identify sections requiring repairs and conduct regular inspections of areas vulnerable to fence failures.\n\nThe operational fleet of the wildlife sector is also being restructured, with 16 double cabs, 84 single cabs, nine field tractors, one hand tractor and three specialised deep-forest vehicles being deployed to Range Forest Offices and Beat Offices."
  },
