    "chunk_size_bytes": 1212,
    "chunk_text": "The operational fleet of the wildlife sector is also being restructured, with 16 double cabs, 84 single cabs, nine field tractors, one hand tractor and three specialised deep-forest vehicles being deployed to Range Forest Offices and Beat Offices.\n\nDedicated cabs have also been provided to regional veterinary units in Pandulagama, Lunugamvehera, Nikaweratiya and Giritale, as well as the Elephant Transit Home.\n\nThe vehicles are intended to strengthen the emergency response capacity of veterinary teams and enable them to reach injured wild elephants more quickly for treatment.\n\nThe Government said the new resources would allow wildlife officers to reach conflict locations promptly, carry out daily inspections of electric fences, protect agricultural lands and property and respond to situations threatening both human and elephant lives.\n\nThe expenditure for the initiative is being funded through the Wildlife Preservation Fund, without placing an additional burden on the Treasury.\n\nThe programme is part of the Government\u2019s broader objective of strengthening wildlife conservation through practical and scientific measures while moving national policy towards sustainable human-elephant coexistence."
