    "md5": "f46fa32ecf4f31c27ea4037ca3e33d99",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "The IMF also recommended developing a medium-term revenue strategy to improve tax collection while making the tax system more efficient and equitable. It called for broadening the tax base, reviewing tax exemptions and incentives, and strengthening revenue administration.\n\nThe Fund further stressed the importance of maintaining the integrity of Sri Lanka\u2019s anti-corruption framework, noting concerns that certain provisions of recently proposed amendments could weaken transparency and accountability.\n\nThe IMF said Sri Lanka\u2019s transition from economic stabilisation to long-term transformation would require continued structural reforms aimed at improving the business environment, attracting investment, liberalising trade, modernising business and labour regulations, expanding access to finance and advancing digitalisation.\n\nDuring the mission, the IMF team also visited Jaffna and held discussions on the Northern Province\u2019s economic potential. The team identified opportunities for investment and job creation in areas including agriculture, fisheries, tourism and renewable energy, while stressing the importance of connectivity, skills development and social protection.\n\nThe IMF delegation held meetings with President and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake, Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya, senior government officials, Central Bank officials, parliamentarians, private-sector representatives, civil society organisations and development partners.\n\nThe IMF said discussions with the Sri Lankan authorities will continue with the aim of reaching a staff-level agreement in the near term, which would pave the way for the timely completion of the Seventh Review under the IMF programme."
