    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-cc6f6937-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ac23a2294ba707de754f2fbfa821fa64",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka lost 10,000 foreign jobs due to corruption, poor handling: SLBFE Chairman\n\nColombo, Sep. 23 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka lost a significant number of foreign employment opportunities due to corrupt practices and poor handling of overseas job opportunities during the previous government, Sri Lanka Bureau of Foreign Employment (SLBFE) Chairman Kosala Wickramasinghe said.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka had received 12,000 agricultural employment opportunities internationally during the previous government but managed to send only around 2,000 workers for those jobs, resulting in the loss of the remaining 10,000 opportunities.\n\nWickramasinghe said various fraudulent and corrupt practices relating to foreign employment had affected Sri Lanka's ability to make proper use of international employment opportunities.\n\nHe said the SLBFE was continuously working to prevent such practices and was taking legal action against corruption involving employment agencies as well as corrupt practices involving individuals connected to such agencies.\n\nHe said that the Bureau's responsibility was not merely to send Sri Lankans abroad for employment but also to regulate the foreign employment process and promote and develop welfare activities for migrant workers.\n\nWickramasinghe made these remarks while addressing a group of Sri Lankans preparing to leave for employment in Israel at the Bureau on September 21.\n\nHe said various ideas and perceptions had developed in Sri Lankan society regarding foreign employment, while Israel had currently become a highly sought-after destination among Sri Lankan workers due to the demand in the Israeli labour market and comparatively high salaries.\n\nHe also rejected the claim that the number of employment opportunities received from the Israeli Government had decreased because such jobs were being channelled through private employment agencies."
  },
